{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GFrCe4rLJ9fzPPtRSDhgfz","type":"tool_result","content":"11\t## 0. Read this first\n12\t\n13\t### 0.1 The title\n14\tPicked the way TASK.md asks. 50 playful riffs on \"American Football\" were brainstormed silently and one was drawn\n15\twith a real random number generator: Python `secrets.randbelow(50)`, which uses OS entropy, gave index 33, which is\n16\t**American Football Fiasco**. It shows on the title screen as `AMERICAN FOOTBALL FIASCO`. Do not re-roll it.\n17\t\n18\t### 0.2 Names and deliverables\n19\t- GameTitle, file-safe: `AmericanFootballFiasco`. SOURCE, file-safe: `AmericanFootball`.\n20\t- The dev folder (all dev files go here, inside this folder):\n21\t  `AmericanFootballFiasco-AmericanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174831/`\n22\t- The deliverable: `AmericanFootballFiasco-AmericanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174831.html` in this\n23\t  folder's root, beside TASK.md. It is one self-contained HTML file and runs from `file://`.\n24\t- `exit-interview.json` beside it, in exactly the shape TASK.md gives. Check that it parses.\n25\t- If your TASK.md gives another model name or timestamp in those brackets, use your names and keep the GameTitle and\n26\t  SOURCE parts.\n27\t\n28\t### 0.3 Working rules (from TASK.md, repeated here so none is missed)\n29\t- Read only the files you need. Other agents share this directory, this machine's ports and its processes.\n30\t- Prefer `file://` and the engine's headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), run\n31\t  `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, read the port it prints, and check that the page it serves is your game\n32\t  before you trust a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID. Never use pkill, killall, `fuser -k`, or\n33\t  kill whatever holds a port.\n34\t- `/tmp` on this machine is a small tmpfs that other agents fill up (it was full during planning). Put clones, caches\n35\t  and test output in your dev folder (`.cache/`, `shots/`, `check-output/`), not in `/tmp`.\n36\t- Tests: one command that prints only failures and a one-line count (§21). Rerun it only after you change code it\n37\t  covers. Take screenshots only for what a script can't check. Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review,\n38\t  judge or audit.\n39\t- Done means no TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features. Every feature in this plan ships, or this plan says how it\n40\t  is cut.\n41\t- The Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback go in the in-game About/Credits screen\n42\t  and in your final chat reply. The same answers go in `exit-interview.json` (§23). Write \"unknown\" for anything you\n43\t  can't confirm. The Plan field is `PLAN.md`.\n44\t\n45\t### 0.4 How to read this plan\n46\t- §1–2: what the game is, and where each TASK.md requirement is covered. §3–4: setup and architecture.\n47\t  §5–17: the systems. §18: the content. §19: build order. §20: risks. §21: testing. §22: tuning defaults. §23: done\n48\t  and hand-off. Appendices: data schemas, the network messages, pose codes, events.\n49\t- Numbers are starting values. The important ones are dev sliders (§17, §22). Tune with the sliders and the Play Lab;\n50\t  don't hand-edit numbers spread through the code.\n51\t- \"Engine change E#\" means modify your copy of the engine (§3.6) and log it in `ENGINE-CHANGES.md`, because the About\n52\t  screen and the exit interview ask what you changed and why.\n53\t\n54\t---\n55\t\n56\t## 1. The game on one page\n57\t\n58\t**Pitch.** The Tumbleweed Junction Fiascos have lost 99 games in a row, and their owner has just bet their field on\n59\tthe Fiasco Cup, the most over-the-top football tournament on Earth. It is 7-on-7 arcade football: turbo sprints,\n60\tbone-rattling Big Hits, flaming Hot Streaks, trick plays, mascots running onto the field, laterals across the field\n61\tand announcers losing their minds, all in pixel art drawn by code, with gibberish voices.\n62\t\n63\t**Core loop (write it as one sentence at the top of `src/00-boot.js`):** *Call a play from big cards, snap, then run,\n64\tpass, juke and hit your way down the field in a 5–10 second burst of chaos, cheer the replay, and do it again before\n65\tthe clock runs out.*\n66\t\n67\t| | |\n68\t|---|---|\n69\t| Genres | Arcade sports (American football), with beat-'em-up hit feel and a light story campaign |\n70\t| Perspective | A sideline **Broadcast** camera: the field runs left to right, seen from above the near sideline (a custom view, pitch 50°). A rotated **Kick Cam** behind the kicker for field goals and PATs. Replay angles: low field level, isometric, reverse. Optional Sky and Blimp views on V. |\n71\t| Players | 1–4 local (couch: pads and shared keyboard), or 1–4 online in AI Hub sessions (shared world) |\n72\t| Modes | Story (THE FIASCO CUP: prologue plus 5 chapters), Exhibition, Practice (the tutorial drills), Hall of Fiasco (records), Options, About/Credits, the attract mode, the Dev mode (from the pause menu) |\n73\t| Length | Story about 45 minutes. An exhibition about 12 minutes with 2:00 quarters. A hub round about 5–6 minutes (one half). |\n74\t| Tone | A 1990s arcade sports broadcast turned to 11: hype-man announcers, small-town underdog movie, trash talk, puns, harmless cartoon violence (PG) |\n75\t| Look | my-3D2dge's native style: HD Humanoid rigs drawn as pixel art, `res: 'ps1'` with `expand`, lit scenes, weather, particles |\n76\t\n77\t**What makes it the source (the pillars every feature serves):**\n78\t1. **The play call and the snap.** Football's rhythm: a strategic beat (cards), then an explosive physical burst.\n79\t2. **Big plays that feel huge.** Hit-stop, shake, slow motion, comic text, the crowd on its feet, the announcers\n80\t   screaming, a replay.\n81\t3. **Spectacle.** Stadiums, crowds, cheerleaders, mascots, marching band, pyrotechnics, weather, night lights.\n82\t4. **Underdogs and trash talk.** Cartoon characters with big personalities and a clear villain.\n83\t5. **Couch-to-online competition.** Head-to-head and co-op from 1 to 4 players.\n84\t\n85\t**Fiasco rules** (arcade simplifications, said on the loading tips and the Help screen): 7 per side; 4 downs to gain\n86\t**20 yards**; no penalties; late hits allowed (and celebrated); laterals any time; turbo for everyone.\n87\t\n88\t---\n89\t\n90\t## 2. TASK.md coverage map\n91\t\n92\t| TASK.md asks for | Where in this plan |\n93\t|---|---|\n94\t| Genre and perspective that best fit | §1, §5.6, §11.1 |\n95\t| Title from 50 brainstormed with a real RNG | §0.1 |\n96\t| Single HTML file; CDN deps allowed (none needed) | §3.5 |\n97\t| Built on the engine handoff, which may be changed | §3, §3.6 |\n98\t| Engine's native art style plus new assets for the source | §12 |\n99\t| Engine's native animation plus new animations | §12.3–12.5 |\n100\t| Audio all in code; Animalese/Simlish voices | §13 |\n101\t| Online through AI Hub, and the game on its own | §16 |\n102\t| Dev subfolder; bundle in the root; servers and processes etiquette | §0.2, §0.3, §3 |\n103\t| Performance unlimited (detail, effects, lights, shadows) | §11.5, §12.7 |\n104\t| Headless tests, scripted whole-game playthrough, one command | §21 |\n105\t| Feature-complete demo showing every mechanic; vertical-slice infrastructure | §15, §18, §19 |\n106\t| Dev mode from the pause menu; live sliders with reset to defaults | §17 |\n107\t| Attract mode after 10 s idle; AI may cheat; any input exits | §15.4 |\n108\t| Multiplayer through the hub protocol | §16 |\n109\t| Narrative tone like the source | §15.1, §18.6 |\n110\t| 10-foot, controller-first UI; native keyboard and mouse; device-following prompts; PS glyphs plus an Xbox option; no dense web screens | §9, §14 |\n111\t| Touch-only: \"Controller or keyboard required\", the Settings button, attract runs | §14.12 |\n112\t| No TODOs or stubs | §0.3, §23 |\n113\t| Source Information, feedback, About screen, exit-interview.json | §14.10, §23 |\n114\t\n115\t---\n116\t\n117\t## 3. Tech stack and setup\n118\t\n119\t### 3.1 What you build on\n120\t- **Engine:** my-3D2dge **v0.85.2**, the handoff package (`dist/handoff/` of github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge,"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c28e7abf-db6e-401a-a911-43ba9983a968","uuid":"148789ea-5263-4c04-bbe3-b32ecf8c5df7","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:47:26.672Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174831__x898ed5/work/PLAN.md","content":"## 0. Read this first\n\n### 0.1 The title\nPicked the way TASK.md asks. 50 playful riffs on \"American Football\" were brainstormed silently and one was drawn\nwith a real random number generator: Python `secrets.randbelow(50)`, which uses OS entropy, gave index 33, which is\n**American Football Fiasco**. It shows on the title screen as `AMERICAN FOOTBALL FIASCO`. Do not re-roll it.\n\n### 0.2 Names and deliverables\n- GameTitle, file-safe: `AmericanFootballFiasco`. SOURCE, file-safe: `AmericanFootball`.\n- The dev folder (all dev files go here, inside this folder):\n  `AmericanFootballFiasco-AmericanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174831/`\n- The deliverable: `AmericanFootballFiasco-AmericanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174831.html` in this\n  folder's root, beside TASK.md. It is one self-contained HTML file and runs from `file://`.\n- `exit-interview.json` beside it, in exactly the shape TASK.md gives. Check that it parses.\n- If your TASK.md gives another model name or timestamp in those brackets, use your names and keep the GameTitle and\n  SOURCE parts.\n\n### 0.3 Working rules (from TASK.md, repeated here so none is missed)\n- Read only the files you need. Other agents share this directory, this machine's ports and its processes.\n- Prefer `file://` and the engine's headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), run\n  `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, read the port it prints, and check that the page it serves is your game\n  before you trust a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID. Never use pkill, killall, `fuser -k`, or\n  kill whatever holds a port.\n- `/tmp` on this machine is a small tmpfs that other agents fill up (it was full during planning). Put clones, caches\n  and test output in your dev folder (`.cache/`, `shots/`, `check-output/`), not in `/tmp`.\n- Tests: one command that prints only failures and a one-line count (§21). Rerun it only after you change code it\n  covers. Take screenshots only for what a script can't check. Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review,\n  judge or audit.\n- Done means no TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features. Every feature in this plan ships, or this plan says how it\n  is cut.\n- The Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback go in the in-game About/Credits screen\n  and in your final chat reply. The same answers go in `exit-interview.json` (§23). Write \"unknown\" for anything you\n  can't confirm. The Plan field is `PLAN.md`.\n\n### 0.4 How to read this plan\n- §1–2: what the game is, and where each TASK.md requirement is covered. §3–4: setup and architecture.\n  §5–17: the systems. §18: the content. §19: build order. §20: risks. §21: testing. §22: tuning defaults. §23: done\n  and hand-off. Appendices: data schemas, the network messages, pose codes, events.\n- Numbers are starting values. The important ones are dev sliders (§17, §22). Tune with the sliders and the Play Lab;\n  don't hand-edit numbers spread through the code.\n- \"Engine change E#\" means modify your copy of the engine (§3.6) and log it in `ENGINE-CHANGES.md`, because the About\n  screen and the exit interview ask what you changed and why.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game on one page\n\n**Pitch.** The Tumbleweed Junction Fiascos have lost 99 games in a row, and their owner has just bet their field on\nthe Fiasco Cup, the most over-the-top football tournament on Earth. It is 7-on-7 arcade football: turbo sprints,\nbone-rattling Big Hits, flaming Hot Streaks, trick plays, mascots running onto the field, laterals across the field\nand announcers losing their minds, all in pixel art drawn by code, with gibberish voices.\n\n**Core loop (write it as one sentence at the top of `src/00-boot.js`):** *Call a play from big cards, snap, then run,\npass, juke and hit your way down the field in a 5–10 second burst of chaos, cheer the replay, and do it again before\nthe clock runs out.*\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Genres | Arcade sports (American football), with beat-'em-up hit feel and a light story campaign |\n| Perspective | A sideline **Broadcast** camera: the field runs left to right, seen from above the near sideline (a custom view, pitch 50°). A rotated **Kick Cam** behind the kicker for field goals and PATs. Replay angles: low field level, isometric, reverse. Optional Sky and Blimp views on V. |\n| Players | 1–4 local (couch: pads and shared keyboard), or 1–4 online in AI Hub sessions (shared world) |\n| Modes | Story (THE FIASCO CUP: prologue plus 5 chapters), Exhibition, Practice (the tutorial drills), Hall of Fiasco (records), Options, About/Credits, the attract mode, the Dev mode (from the pause menu) |\n| Length | Story about 45 minutes. An exhibition about 12 minutes with 2:00 quarters. A hub round about 5–6 minutes (one half). |\n| Tone | A 1990s arcade sports broadcast turned to 11: hype-man announcers, small-town underdog movie, trash talk, puns, harmless cartoon violence (PG) |\n| Look | my-3D2dge's native style: HD Humanoid rigs drawn as pixel art, `res: 'ps1'` with `expand`, lit scenes, weather, particles |\n\n**What makes it the source (the pillars every feature serves):**\n1. **The play call and the snap.** Football's rhythm: a strategic beat (cards), then an explosive physical burst.\n2. **Big plays that feel huge.** Hit-stop, shake, slow motion, comic text, the crowd on its feet, the announcers\n   screaming, a replay.\n3. **Spectacle.** Stadiums, crowds, cheerleaders, mascots, marching band, pyrotechnics, weather, night lights.\n4. **Underdogs and trash talk.** Cartoon characters with big personalities and a clear villain.\n5. **Couch-to-online competition.** Head-to-head and co-op from 1 to 4 players.\n\n**Fiasco rules** (arcade simplifications, said on the loading tips and the Help screen): 7 per side; 4 downs to gain\n**20 yards**; no penalties; late hits allowed (and celebrated); laterals any time; turbo for everyone.\n\n---\n\n## 2. TASK.md coverage map\n\n| TASK.md asks for | Where in this plan |\n|---|---|\n| Genre and perspective that best fit | §1, §5.6, §11.1 |\n| Title from 50 brainstormed with a real RNG | §0.1 |\n| Single HTML file; CDN deps allowed (none needed) | §3.5 |\n| Built on the engine handoff, which may be changed | §3, §3.6 |\n| Engine's native art style plus new assets for the source | §12 |\n| Engine's native animation plus new animations | §12.3–12.5 |\n| Audio all in code; Animalese/Simlish voices | §13 |\n| Online through AI Hub, and the game on its own | §16 |\n| Dev subfolder; bundle in the root; servers and processes etiquette | §0.2, §0.3, §3 |\n| Performance unlimited (detail, effects, lights, shadows) | §11.5, §12.7 |\n| Headless tests, scripted whole-game playthrough, one command | §21 |\n| Feature-complete demo showing every mechanic; vertical-slice infrastructure | §15, §18, §19 |\n| Dev mode from the pause menu; live sliders with reset to defaults | §17 |\n| Attract mode after 10 s idle; AI may cheat; any input exits | §15.4 |\n| Multiplayer through the hub protocol | §16 |\n| Narrative tone like the source | §15.1, §18.6 |\n| 10-foot, controller-first UI; native keyboard and mouse; device-following prompts; PS glyphs plus an Xbox option; no dense web screens | §9, §14 |\n| Touch-only: \"Controller or keyboard required\", the Settings button, attract runs | §14.12 |\n| No TODOs or stubs | §0.3, §23 |\n| Source Information, feedback, About screen, exit-interview.json | §14.10, §23 |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Tech stack and setup\n\n### 3.1 What you build on\n- **Engine:** my-3D2dge **v0.85.2**, the handoff package (`dist/handoff/` of github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge,","numLines":110,"startLine":11,"totalLines":2454}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01GFrCe4rLJ9fzPPtRSDhgfz","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
