{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DNDHoPEK7nP6hodfEuSRrn","type":"tool_result","content":"120\t- **Engine:** my-3D2dge **v0.85.2**, the handoff package (`dist/handoff/` of github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge,\n121\t  commit `4390a9852fe0ad01e1699a160f2a331931bb1a72`, 2026-11-03). `my-3d2dge.js` there is the full engine with every\n122\t  module (1.2 MB, readable). Read `API.md` whole. Grep `API-INDEX.md` for names. Read the parts of `AI_GUIDE.md`\n123\t  that §4 points to, and `docs/ASSETS.md` whole before writing shapes. Copy patterns from `kits/sports.js` (the\n124\t  SPORTS kit: the crowd on the stands, goalposts drawn with `r.queue`, the ball's shadow, `game.allowJoin`, the radio\n125\t  commentary through `E.Dialog`) and `game/test.mjs` (the headless test).\n126\t- **AI Hub client:** `lib/hub/session.js` **1.4.0** from github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub (commit\n127\t  `f8835e4824fbf9bbb4bac3a35e88e3ab197fdd1e`). Paste it unmodified. Read `lib/hub/README.md` whole, and\n128\t  `lib/hub/examples/arena.html` for the shared-world pattern this plan follows.\n129\t- **Motion clips:** the repository's `src/mocap/readable.js` and `src/mocap/mocap.js` (the runtime, 49 KB) and a set\n130\t  cut with `tools/anim-set.mjs` (§12.4). Clips are CC0 (Quaternius, Mesh2Motion) and free for any use (CMU). Credit\n131\t  them on the About screen.\n132\t- **No other runtime libraries.** Canvas 2D and Web Audio through the engine.\n133\t- **Tooling:** Node 24 (v24.22.1 here), Playwright **1.63.0** (pinned by the handoff's `package.json`; Chromium 1243 is\n134\t  already in `~/.cache/ms-playwright`), Python 3 for the local server.\n135\t\n136\t### 3.2 Fetching (sparse, into your dev folder)\n137\tThe full engine repository is large (its `examples/` alone is 79 MB) and the AI Hub repository is over 1 GB. Fetch\n138\tonly what you need:\n139\t```sh\n140\tcd AmericanFootballFiasco-AmericanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174831\n141\tmkdir -p .cache && cd .cache\n142\tgit clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git engine\n143\tgit -C engine sparse-checkout set dist/handoff src/mocap tools\n144\tgit clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub.git hub\n145\tgit -C hub sparse-checkout set lib/hub\n146\tgit -C engine log -1 --format=%H; git -C hub log -1 --format=%H   # record them in ENGINE-CHANGES.md\n147\t```\n148\tIf HEAD has moved past the commits above, check that `dist/handoff/README.md` still says v0.85.2 and `session.js`\n149\tsays 1.4.0. If they don't, fetch the pinned commits (`git fetch --depth 1 origin <sha>` then `git checkout <sha>`).\n150\t`.cache/` is never bundled and may be deleted at the end.\n151\t\n152\t### 3.3 The dev folder\n153\t```\n154\tAmericanFootballFiasco-AmericanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174831/\n155\t  package.json         type: module; scripts: test, build, shots; devDependencies: playwright 1.63.0\n156\t  index.html           the dev page: loads vendor/ then src/ in order (open with file://)\n157\t  build.mjs            the bundler: writes ../<deliverable>.html and ../exit-interview.json\n158\t  ENGINE-CHANGES.md    every engine change: what, where, why (feeds the About screen)\n159\t  vendor/\n160\t    my-3d2dge.js       the engine (your modified copy of dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js)\n161\t    session.js         the AI Hub client 1.4.0, byte for byte\n162\t    mocap/readable.js  mocap/mocap.js   from the engine repository's src/mocap\n163\t    anim/fiasco-anim.js                 generated (§12.4)\n164\t    assets/fiasco-store.js              generated: the few store props used (§12.6)\n165\t  src/                 the game, one file per module, numbered in load order (§4.3)\n166\t  tools/               copied from the handoff: check.mjs filmstrip.mjs replay.mjs asset-find.mjs asset-sheet.mjs\n167\t                       asset-lib.mjs asset-set.mjs; from the repository: anim-set.mjs mocap-lib.mjs\n168\t  test/                run.mjs (the one command), lib/, suites/, browser/, hub/ (harness.html bridge.js round.js)\n169\t  shots/               screenshots (output only)\n170\t```\n171\t\n172\t### 3.4 The dev page\n173\t`index.html` copies the handoff template's page: the `e3-boot` splash markup at the start of `<body>`, the canvas\n174\t`#screen`, then one `<script src>` per file. `session.js` goes first in `<head>`, right after `<meta charset>`\n175\t(§16.2). Next come the `hub:` meta tags, then in the body the engine, mocap/readable.js, mocap/mocap.js, the anim\n176\tset, the store props, and `src/*.js` in order. Opening it from `file://` must play the whole game.\n177\t\n178\t### 3.5 The bundle (`node build.mjs`)\n179\t1. Read `index.html`. Replace each `<script src=\"X\"></script>` with `<script>` + the file's text + `</script>`.\n180\t   Escape every `</script` inside a file's text as `<\\/script` (the engine and the anim set contain strings that need\n181\t   it). Keep `session.js` byte-identical apart from that wrapper, and keep it the first script in `<head>`.\n182\t2. Inline `src/about.json` as `window.FF_ABOUT = {...}`, and write `../exit-interview.json` from the same object\n183\t   (§23), so the About screen and the file can never disagree.\n184\t3. Write `../AmericanFootballFiasco-AmericanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174831.html`. Expect about 2 MB.\n185\t4. Print the size and the SHA-256 of the bundled `session.js` part, and check that it matches the vendor file.\n186\t\n187\tThe bundle needs no network. Don't use CDN dependencies: everything is already local.\n188\t\n189\t### 3.6 Engine changes to make (log each in ENGINE-CHANGES.md)\n190\tKeep the changes small and general. Each one is an engine feature a future game would want too.\n191\t\n192\t| # | Change | Where (in `my-3d2dge.js` v0.85.2) | Why |\n193\t|---|---|---|---|\n194\t| E1 | Add the glyphs `✕ ○ □ △` to the 5×7 font `G57` and the 3×5 `GLYPHS` | the PIXEL FONT section (search `const G57 =`) | `input.label()` returns `'✕'` and similar for a PlayStation pad, but the pixel font draws `?` for them |\n195\t| E2 | The `glyphs` setting defaults to `'playstation'`, its options are `[['playstation','PlayStation'],['xbox','Xbox']]`, and it shows on the Settings screen under a \"Controller\" heading as well as on the Controls screen | the standard settings table (search `glyphs: { group: 'Controller'`) and `Shell._settingsRows` | TASK.md: PlayStation glyphs, with a Settings option for Xbox |\n196\t| E3 | A **gear hook** on the HD Humanoid: `o.gear(api)` called in `_drawHD` just before `ops.sort(...)`, with `api = { g, view, Sp, S, Q, J, C, u, cap, limb, push(d, fn), rig }` | `_drawHD` (search `ops.sort((a, b) => a.d - b.d)`, the first one) | Facemasks, shoulder pads, jersey numbers, the carried ball, pom-poms and mascot heads, depth-sorted inside the rig |\n197\t| E4 | A `'football'` hat style in `Humanoid._hat`: a shell 1.12× the head, a center stripe (`h.stripe`), an ear logo dot (`h.logo`), and a grey facemask of 2 bars plus a vertical bar in front of the face | `_hat` (search `st === 'helmet'`) | A helmet that reads as football from every view |\n198\t| E5 | `E.Dialog`'s `voice` may be a function: `voice(ch, i, text, dialog)` is called for each newly typed letter instead of `audio.sfx(voice)` | `Dialog.update` (search `if (this.voice && Math.floor(this.shown) > prev`) | The Animalese voice synth (§13.4) |\n199\t| E6 | Audio buses: `audio.bus(name)` returns a GainNode under the master that follows mute and volume, and its volume follows a setting (`voices`, `crowd`) | the AUDIO section, after `_init` | Voices and the crowd bed need their own sliders and long-lived nodes |\n200\t| E7 | `Shell.show(name, { rows: [settingKeys] })`: any screen may list settings rows, and settings in a group whose name starts with `Dev` never show on the standard Settings screen | `Shell._render` (search `if (name === 'settings') this._settingsRows(add);`) and `_settingsRows` | The Dev mode's live sliders (§17) reuse the engine's slider rows |\n201\t| E8 | `game.menu('settings', { pause: false })` opens Settings without pausing | where `game.menu` is defined | In a hub session the world must not freeze when one player opens Settings (§16.6) |\n202\t| E9 | 10-foot Shell theme: an option `new E.Shell(game, { scale: 1.6 })` that scales the Shell's CSS (fonts, buttons, sliders, panel widths) by a factor, with `clamp()` by viewport | `SHELL_CSS` and the Shell constructor (or inject your own stylesheet with selectors stronger than `.esh`) | The standard pause, settings and controls screens are built for a desk at 14 px. A TV at 10 feet needs about 24 px or more. |\n203\t\n204\tAnything else you change, log it the same way. After the engine changes, `node tools/check.mjs` on the handoff's\n205\t`kits/sports.html` must still pass. Keep a copy of the untouched handoff in `.cache/` to diff against.\n206\t\n207\t---\n208\t\n209\t## 4. Architecture\n210\t\n211\t### 4.1 Principles\n212\t1. **The sim and the presentation are split.** The match simulation (`FF.sim`) owns athletes, the ball, rules and the\n213\t   clock in world units. It never reads a rig, a camera or the DOM. The presentation (rigs, camera, HUD, FX,\n214\t   commentary, audio) reads the sim's state and its events. This gives fast headless tests (presentation off), network\n215\t   clients that only render, and replays.\n216\t2. **Everything that controls an athlete makes intents.** Humans (pad, keyboard, mouse), the AI, network clients, the\n217\t   attract director and test bots all produce the same intent object per athlete per step (Appendix A.6). The sim\n218\t   consumes intents and nothing else.\n219\t3. **Content is data.** Teams, rosters, formations, plays, stadiums, cards, story chapters, cutscenes, drills,\n220\t   commentary lines and tuning are plain data objects in `src/1x-data-*.js`, checked by a validation test. A designer\n221\t   adds a team, a play or a chapter without touching code. This is the \"built out later with normal designer tools\"\n222\t   requirement.\n223\t4. **One scenario system.** A *scenario* (Appendix A.5) sets up any game situation: stadium, teams, score, clock,\n224\t   down and distance, weather, cheats and a scripted sequence. Story chapters, practice drills, the attract mode, the\n225\t   Dev mode's situation jumper and the tests all use it.\n226\t5. **Events, not hooks everywhere.** The sim emits events on `FF.bus` (Appendix D). Commentary, the crowd, FX,\n227\t   replays, stats, the Fiasco Meter, Hot Streak, cards and boss specials listen. Adding a card never touches the\n228\t   tackle code: the tackle code asks the bus for modifiers (`FF.bus.mod('tackle.p', value, ctx)`).\n229\t6. **Deterministic.** The engine's fixed 1/120 s step. All gameplay randomness comes from `FF.rng.sim` (a seeded\n230\t   `E.rng`) and cosmetic randomness from `FF.rng.fx`. Never call `Math.random` in the sim. With the same seed and\n231\t   inputs, a match plays out the same. Tests rely on this.\n232\t\n233\t### 4.2 Namespace and file pattern\n234\t`src/00-boot.js` creates `window.FF = { E: My3D2dge, game, rng, bus, data: {}, ... }`. Every other file is an IIFE:\n235\t`(() => { 'use strict'; const E = My3D2dge, FF = window.FF; ... FF.Crowd = ...; })();`. Files only read what earlier\n236\tfiles defined, and `90-main.js` starts the game. In Node tests, `window` is the vm context (kits/test.mjs pattern).\n237\tGuard every browser-only global (`matchMedia`, `navigator`, `document`) with `typeof` checks.\n238\t\n239\t### 4.3 Modules (load order)\n240\t\n241\t| File | Responsibility | Key exports |\n242\t|---|---|---|\n243\t| `00-boot.js` | the `E.Game` (`res: 'ps1', expand: true`, the menu options: Dev mode item, `quit: 'title'` or false in sessions), the input map `FF.KEYS` (§9.1), settings definitions (§14.11), the RNG streams, the bus, hub detection (`FF.inHub = !!(window.AIHub && AIHub.active)`) | `FF.game`, `FF.KEYS`, `FF.rng`, `FF.bus` |\n244\t| `01-util.js` | math (yards ↔ world), angle helpers, intercept solver, segment distance, weighted pick, line-bank picker with no repeats, compact encoders for net | `FF.u` |\n245\t| `02-tuning.js` | every tunable with its default, range and group (§22), registered as `Dev …` settings (E7); `FF.T.get(key)` | `FF.T` |\n246\t| `10-data-teams.js` | 6 teams: colors, logos, rosters, stats, looks, mascots, coaches, AI personalities, stars and specials (§18.1) | `FF.data.teams` |\n247\t| `11-data-plays.js` | formations, offensive, defensive and special plays, kickoff and PAT choices (§18.3) | `FF.data.formations`, `.plays`, `.dplays` |\n248\t| `12-data-stadiums.js` | 5 stadiums (§18.2) | `FF.data.stadiums` |\n249\t| `13-data-cards.js` | 8 Fiasco Cards with their effects as bus listeners (§10.2) | `FF.data.cards` |\n250\t| `14-data-story.js` | chapters, cutscene scripts (E.Story nodes), halftime choices, rewards, loss lines (§15.1, §18.6) | `FF.data.story` |\n251\t| `15-data-drills.js` | the tutorial and practice drills (§15.2) | `FF.data.drills` |\n252\t| `16-data-lines.js` | commentary line banks per event, refs, QB cadences, crowd chants (§18.5) | `FF.data.lines` |\n253\t| `17-data-attract.js` | the attract director's segments (§15.4) | `FF.data.attract` |\n254\t| `20-audio-voice.js` | the babble voice synth and the speaker profiles (§13.4) | `FF.voice` |\n255\t| `21-audio-music.js` | songs, layers, stings, the sfx definitions, the crowd bed (§13) | `FF.music`, `FF.sfx`, `FF.crowdAudio` |\n256\t| `22-glyphs.js` | button chips per device and glyph setting; prompt strings (§9.3) | `FF.glyph` |\n257\t| `23-ui-kit.js` | 10-foot canvas UI: a focus grid with mouse hover, big buttons, cards, tabs, panels, comic bursts, the prompt strip, captions, toasts | `FF.ui` |\n258\t| `24-shapes.js` | the game's own `E.SHAPES` entries (§12.6) and its materials | — |\n259\t| `25-field.js` | the stadium builder: TileMap, floor painting, stands, goalposts, pylons, markers, sideline dressing, lights, weather (§5) | `FF.Stadium` |\n260\t| `26-crowd.js` | crowd agents, excitement, waves, signs, flashes (§11.4) | `FF.Crowd` |\n261\t| `27-rigs.js` | athlete and cast rig factory, the gear hook (§12.2), pose codes to rig drive (§12.3), the mocap player, PoseFX | `FF.Rigs` |\n262\t| `28-ball.js` | the football: held, thrown, kicked, loose; drawing the spiral, shadow and trails (§7.6) | `FF.Ball` |\n263\t| `30-sim.js` | the match: the state machine, rules, clock, possession, spotting (§6) | `FF.sim` |\n264\t| `31-sim-athletes.js` | athletes, movement, turbo, collisions, blocking contact (§7.1–7.4, §7.9) | |\n265\t| `32-sim-actions.js` | actions and their resolution: throw, catch, tackle, Big Hit, dive, spin, stiff arm, hurdle, strip, lateral, handoff, kick, late hit, pile-up (§7.5–7.8) | |\n266\t| `33-ai.js` | athlete brains and behaviors (§8.1–8.4) | `FF.ai` |\n267\t| `34-ai-coach.js` | CPU play calling, cards, 4th down, clock, difficulty, COACH SAYS (§8.5) | `FF.coach` |\n268\t| `35-control.js` | human control: assignment, switching, intents from devices, the mouse pointer on the field (§9) | `FF.control` |\n269\t| `36-ots.js` | the over-the-top systems: Hot Streak, Fiasco Meter, cards in play, boss specials, Dinged, helmets popping off, mascots, refs, lights-out (§10) | `FF.ots` |\n270\t| `37-camera.js` | the camera director and views (§11.1) | `FF.cam` |\n271\t| `38-replay.js` | the recorder, replay player and highlight reel (§11.2) | `FF.replay` |\n272\t| `39-commentary.js` | the announcer and on-field text pops (§11.3) | `FF.talk` |\n273\t| `40-fx.js` | hit FX, divots, confetti, fireworks, pyro, breath puffs, fire trails (§11.5) | `FF.fx` |\n274\t| `41-hud.js` | score bug, turbo, meters, prompts, captions, event text, receiver chips, kick meter (§14.6) | `FF.hud` |\n275\t| `50-scene-title.js` | title, attract trigger, touch-only gate | scene `title` |\n276\t| `51-scene-menus.js` | main menu, exhibition setup, controller assignment, team and stadium select, Hall of Fiasco, About/Credits | scenes `menu`, `setup`, `records`, `about` |\n277\t| `52-scene-match.js` | the match scene, which ties sim, control, AI, camera, HUD, play call, kicking, halftime, results and pause items together | scene `match` |\n278\t| `53-playcall.js` | the play-call UI (§14.5) | `FF.PlayCall` |\n279\t| `54-scene-story.js` | the story hub (the bracket board), the cutscene player, chapter intros and outros, rewards, ending and credits | scenes `story`, `cutscene`, `credits` |\n280\t| `55-scene-drills.js` | tutorial and practice drills | scene `drill` |\n281\t| `56-scene-attract.js` | the attract director and its overlay | scene `attract` |\n282\t| `57-scene-hubcard.js` | the AI Hub card and countdown (§16.3) | scene `hubcard` |\n283\t| `70-dev.js` | Dev mode: screens, sliders, toggles, situations, Play Lab, overlays (§17) | `FF.dev` |\n284\t| `80-net.js` | the AI Hub adapter: roles, messages, snapshots, prediction, loopback for tests (§16) | `FF.net` |\n285\t| `90-main.js` | registers the scenes, the boot steps, deep links (`#match`, `#drill/passing`, `#attract`, `#story/ch3`), `game.debug`, `game.start` | |\n286\t\n287\t### 4.4 Scenes and the flow between them\n288\t```\n289\tboot ─▶ (AIHub.active?) ─yes─▶ hubcard ─▶ match(session) ─▶ (hub ends the round)\n290\t  └─no─▶ title ─(10 s idle)─▶ attract ─(any input)─▶ title\n291\t           └─▶ menu ─▶ story ─▶ cutscene ⇄ match / drill ─▶ credits\n292\t                    ├▶ setup (exhibition) ─▶ match ─▶ results (inside match) ─▶ menu\n293\t                    ├▶ drill (practice)   ├▶ records   ├▶ about   └▶ Options (game.menu('settings'))\n294\t```\n295\tGameplay scenes (`match`, `drill`) are `pausable: true`, except in hub sessions (§16.6). Their pause menu shows Resume,\n296\t**Dev Mode**, **Restart** (exhibition and practice only), **Quit to Menu**, then the standard Settings, Controls, Full\n297\tscreen and Help. Titles, menus and cutscenes aren't pausable, but the corner menu button opens Settings there.\n298\t\n299\t### 4.5 One step of a match (`match.update(dt)`)\n300\t1. `FF.control.update(dt)`: device input to intents for human-held athletes, including mouse picks.\n301\t2. `FF.ai.update(dt)`: AI brains think at 20 Hz, staggered by athlete index, and emit intents every step from their\n302\t   current behavior.\n303\t3. In a hub session on the host, `FF.net.applyRemote()` writes remote players' intents and predicted positions.\n304\t4. `FF.sim.step(dt, intents)`: phase timers, athlete movement and actions, collisions and blocks, the ball, contacts\n305\t   (tackles, catches, recoveries), boundaries, rules and the clock. It emits events.\n306\t5. `FF.ots.update(dt)`, then `FF.replay.record()`.\n307\t6. Presentation, skipped headless when `FF.fast` is set: `FF.Rigs.update(dt)`, `FF.cam.update(dt)`,\n308\t   `FF.Crowd.update(dt)`, `FF.talk.update(dt)`, `FF.hud.update(dt)`.\n309\t7. In a hub session, `FF.net.send()` on its own clock (§16.5).\n310\t\n311\t`draw(r)`: stadium floor and walls, decals (field lines of scrimmage, the pass target marker, divots), shapes and\n312\tprops, the crowd, athletes (`r.actor`, with `{ pick: athleteId }` for the mouse), the ball, refs and sideline cast, FX,\n313\tlights, the HUD in `r.overlay`, the play-call UI, then captions.\n314\t\n315\t### 4.6 The `game.debug` handle (for tests and tools)\n316\t```js\n317\tgame.debug = {\n318\t  snapshot(),            // { scene, mode, phase, clock, quarter, score, down, toGo, los, possession, ball: {state, x, y, z, holder},\n319\t                         //   athletes: [{ id, team, pos, x, y, z, pose, ctl }], controlled: { p1: id, ... }, events: last 10, errors }\n320\t  scenario(spec),        // load a scenario (A.5) now\n321\t  cheat(name, on),       // godMode, infiniteTurbo, forceCatch, forceFumble, forceINT, noTackle, cpuVsCpu, ...\n322\t  playLab(off, def, n),  // run n headless plays of off vs def, return stats (§8.6)\n323\t  skipCutscene(), finishGame(winnerSide), setClock(s), setScore(h, a), giveCard(side, id), setHot(athleteId),\n324\t  fast(on),              // presentation off for speed (headless only)\n325\t};\n326\t```\n327\t`tools/check.mjs` prints `snapshot()` after its run.\n328\t\n329\t---\n330\t\n331\t## 5. World, units and the stadium\n332\t\n333\t### 5.1 Units\n334\t**One yard is one tile, 16 world units.** The engine's own scale is 16 units = 1 m and a 26-unit hero, so players"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c28e7abf-db6e-401a-a911-43ba9983a968","uuid":"1e01bc8d-a9c4-4f59-8f63-fdbf2c29a7ad","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:47:34.220Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174831__x898ed5/work/PLAN.md","content":"- **Engine:** my-3D2dge **v0.85.2**, the handoff package (`dist/handoff/` of github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge,\n  commit `4390a9852fe0ad01e1699a160f2a331931bb1a72`, 2026-11-03). `my-3d2dge.js` there is the full engine with every\n  module (1.2 MB, readable). Read `API.md` whole. Grep `API-INDEX.md` for names. Read the parts of `AI_GUIDE.md`\n  that §4 points to, and `docs/ASSETS.md` whole before writing shapes. Copy patterns from `kits/sports.js` (the\n  SPORTS kit: the crowd on the stands, goalposts drawn with `r.queue`, the ball's shadow, `game.allowJoin`, the radio\n  commentary through `E.Dialog`) and `game/test.mjs` (the headless test).\n- **AI Hub client:** `lib/hub/session.js` **1.4.0** from github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub (commit\n  `f8835e4824fbf9bbb4bac3a35e88e3ab197fdd1e`). Paste it unmodified. Read `lib/hub/README.md` whole, and\n  `lib/hub/examples/arena.html` for the shared-world pattern this plan follows.\n- **Motion clips:** the repository's `src/mocap/readable.js` and `src/mocap/mocap.js` (the runtime, 49 KB) and a set\n  cut with `tools/anim-set.mjs` (§12.4). Clips are CC0 (Quaternius, Mesh2Motion) and free for any use (CMU). Credit\n  them on the About screen.\n- **No other runtime libraries.** Canvas 2D and Web Audio through the engine.\n- **Tooling:** Node 24 (v24.22.1 here), Playwright **1.63.0** (pinned by the handoff's `package.json`; Chromium 1243 is\n  already in `~/.cache/ms-playwright`), Python 3 for the local server.\n\n### 3.2 Fetching (sparse, into your dev folder)\nThe full engine repository is large (its `examples/` alone is 79 MB) and the AI Hub repository is over 1 GB. Fetch\nonly what you need:\n```sh\ncd AmericanFootballFiasco-AmericanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174831\nmkdir -p .cache && cd .cache\ngit clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git engine\ngit -C engine sparse-checkout set dist/handoff src/mocap tools\ngit clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub.git hub\ngit -C hub sparse-checkout set lib/hub\ngit -C engine log -1 --format=%H; git -C hub log -1 --format=%H   # record them in ENGINE-CHANGES.md\n```\nIf HEAD has moved past the commits above, check that `dist/handoff/README.md` still says v0.85.2 and `session.js`\nsays 1.4.0. If they don't, fetch the pinned commits (`git fetch --depth 1 origin <sha>` then `git checkout <sha>`).\n`.cache/` is never bundled and may be deleted at the end.\n\n### 3.3 The dev folder\n```\nAmericanFootballFiasco-AmericanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174831/\n  package.json         type: module; scripts: test, build, shots; devDependencies: playwright 1.63.0\n  index.html           the dev page: loads vendor/ then src/ in order (open with file://)\n  build.mjs            the bundler: writes ../<deliverable>.html and ../exit-interview.json\n  ENGINE-CHANGES.md    every engine change: what, where, why (feeds the About screen)\n  vendor/\n    my-3d2dge.js       the engine (your modified copy of dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js)\n    session.js         the AI Hub client 1.4.0, byte for byte\n    mocap/readable.js  mocap/mocap.js   from the engine repository's src/mocap\n    anim/fiasco-anim.js                 generated (§12.4)\n    assets/fiasco-store.js              generated: the few store props used (§12.6)\n  src/                 the game, one file per module, numbered in load order (§4.3)\n  tools/               copied from the handoff: check.mjs filmstrip.mjs replay.mjs asset-find.mjs asset-sheet.mjs\n                       asset-lib.mjs asset-set.mjs; from the repository: anim-set.mjs mocap-lib.mjs\n  test/                run.mjs (the one command), lib/, suites/, browser/, hub/ (harness.html bridge.js round.js)\n  shots/               screenshots (output only)\n```\n\n### 3.4 The dev page\n`index.html` copies the handoff template's page: the `e3-boot` splash markup at the start of `<body>`, the canvas\n`#screen`, then one `<script src>` per file. `session.js` goes first in `<head>`, right after `<meta charset>`\n(§16.2). Next come the `hub:` meta tags, then in the body the engine, mocap/readable.js, mocap/mocap.js, the anim\nset, the store props, and `src/*.js` in order. Opening it from `file://` must play the whole game.\n\n### 3.5 The bundle (`node build.mjs`)\n1. Read `index.html`. Replace each `<script src=\"X\"></script>` with `<script>` + the file's text + `</script>`.\n   Escape every `</script` inside a file's text as `<\\/script` (the engine and the anim set contain strings that need\n   it). Keep `session.js` byte-identical apart from that wrapper, and keep it the first script in `<head>`.\n2. Inline `src/about.json` as `window.FF_ABOUT = {...}`, and write `../exit-interview.json` from the same object\n   (§23), so the About screen and the file can never disagree.\n3. Write `../AmericanFootballFiasco-AmericanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174831.html`. Expect about 2 MB.\n4. Print the size and the SHA-256 of the bundled `session.js` part, and check that it matches the vendor file.\n\nThe bundle needs no network. Don't use CDN dependencies: everything is already local.\n\n### 3.6 Engine changes to make (log each in ENGINE-CHANGES.md)\nKeep the changes small and general. Each one is an engine feature a future game would want too.\n\n| # | Change | Where (in `my-3d2dge.js` v0.85.2) | Why |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| E1 | Add the glyphs `✕ ○ □ △` to the 5×7 font `G57` and the 3×5 `GLYPHS` | the PIXEL FONT section (search `const G57 =`) | `input.label()` returns `'✕'` and similar for a PlayStation pad, but the pixel font draws `?` for them |\n| E2 | The `glyphs` setting defaults to `'playstation'`, its options are `[['playstation','PlayStation'],['xbox','Xbox']]`, and it shows on the Settings screen under a \"Controller\" heading as well as on the Controls screen | the standard settings table (search `glyphs: { group: 'Controller'`) and `Shell._settingsRows` | TASK.md: PlayStation glyphs, with a Settings option for Xbox |\n| E3 | A **gear hook** on the HD Humanoid: `o.gear(api)` called in `_drawHD` just before `ops.sort(...)`, with `api = { g, view, Sp, S, Q, J, C, u, cap, limb, push(d, fn), rig }` | `_drawHD` (search `ops.sort((a, b) => a.d - b.d)`, the first one) | Facemasks, shoulder pads, jersey numbers, the carried ball, pom-poms and mascot heads, depth-sorted inside the rig |\n| E4 | A `'football'` hat style in `Humanoid._hat`: a shell 1.12× the head, a center stripe (`h.stripe`), an ear logo dot (`h.logo`), and a grey facemask of 2 bars plus a vertical bar in front of the face | `_hat` (search `st === 'helmet'`) | A helmet that reads as football from every view |\n| E5 | `E.Dialog`'s `voice` may be a function: `voice(ch, i, text, dialog)` is called for each newly typed letter instead of `audio.sfx(voice)` | `Dialog.update` (search `if (this.voice && Math.floor(this.shown) > prev`) | The Animalese voice synth (§13.4) |\n| E6 | Audio buses: `audio.bus(name)` returns a GainNode under the master that follows mute and volume, and its volume follows a setting (`voices`, `crowd`) | the AUDIO section, after `_init` | Voices and the crowd bed need their own sliders and long-lived nodes |\n| E7 | `Shell.show(name, { rows: [settingKeys] })`: any screen may list settings rows, and settings in a group whose name starts with `Dev` never show on the standard Settings screen | `Shell._render` (search `if (name === 'settings') this._settingsRows(add);`) and `_settingsRows` | The Dev mode's live sliders (§17) reuse the engine's slider rows |\n| E8 | `game.menu('settings', { pause: false })` opens Settings without pausing | where `game.menu` is defined | In a hub session the world must not freeze when one player opens Settings (§16.6) |\n| E9 | 10-foot Shell theme: an option `new E.Shell(game, { scale: 1.6 })` that scales the Shell's CSS (fonts, buttons, sliders, panel widths) by a factor, with `clamp()` by viewport | `SHELL_CSS` and the Shell constructor (or inject your own stylesheet with selectors stronger than `.esh`) | The standard pause, settings and controls screens are built for a desk at 14 px. A TV at 10 feet needs about 24 px or more. |\n\nAnything else you change, log it the same way. After the engine changes, `node tools/check.mjs` on the handoff's\n`kits/sports.html` must still pass. Keep a copy of the untouched handoff in `.cache/` to diff against.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Architecture\n\n### 4.1 Principles\n1. **The sim and the presentation are split.** The match simulation (`FF.sim`) owns athletes, the ball, rules and the\n   clock in world units. It never reads a rig, a camera or the DOM. The presentation (rigs, camera, HUD, FX,\n   commentary, audio) reads the sim's state and its events. This gives fast headless tests (presentation off), network\n   clients that only render, and replays.\n2. **Everything that controls an athlete makes intents.** Humans (pad, keyboard, mouse), the AI, network clients, the\n   attract director and test bots all produce the same intent object per athlete per step (Appendix A.6). The sim\n   consumes intents and nothing else.\n3. **Content is data.** Teams, rosters, formations, plays, stadiums, cards, story chapters, cutscenes, drills,\n   commentary lines and tuning are plain data objects in `src/1x-data-*.js`, checked by a validation test. A designer\n   adds a team, a play or a chapter without touching code. This is the \"built out later with normal designer tools\"\n   requirement.\n4. **One scenario system.** A *scenario* (Appendix A.5) sets up any game situation: stadium, teams, score, clock,\n   down and distance, weather, cheats and a scripted sequence. Story chapters, practice drills, the attract mode, the\n   Dev mode's situation jumper and the tests all use it.\n5. **Events, not hooks everywhere.** The sim emits events on `FF.bus` (Appendix D). Commentary, the crowd, FX,\n   replays, stats, the Fiasco Meter, Hot Streak, cards and boss specials listen. Adding a card never touches the\n   tackle code: the tackle code asks the bus for modifiers (`FF.bus.mod('tackle.p', value, ctx)`).\n6. **Deterministic.** The engine's fixed 1/120 s step. All gameplay randomness comes from `FF.rng.sim` (a seeded\n   `E.rng`) and cosmetic randomness from `FF.rng.fx`. Never call `Math.random` in the sim. With the same seed and\n   inputs, a match plays out the same. Tests rely on this.\n\n### 4.2 Namespace and file pattern\n`src/00-boot.js` creates `window.FF = { E: My3D2dge, game, rng, bus, data: {}, ... }`. Every other file is an IIFE:\n`(() => { 'use strict'; const E = My3D2dge, FF = window.FF; ... FF.Crowd = ...; })();`. Files only read what earlier\nfiles defined, and `90-main.js` starts the game. In Node tests, `window` is the vm context (kits/test.mjs pattern).\nGuard every browser-only global (`matchMedia`, `navigator`, `document`) with `typeof` checks.\n\n### 4.3 Modules (load order)\n\n| File | Responsibility | Key exports |\n|---|---|---|\n| `00-boot.js` | the `E.Game` (`res: 'ps1', expand: true`, the menu options: Dev mode item, `quit: 'title'` or false in sessions), the input map `FF.KEYS` (§9.1), settings definitions (§14.11), the RNG streams, the bus, hub detection (`FF.inHub = !!(window.AIHub && AIHub.active)`) | `FF.game`, `FF.KEYS`, `FF.rng`, `FF.bus` |\n| `01-util.js` | math (yards ↔ world), angle helpers, intercept solver, segment distance, weighted pick, line-bank picker with no repeats, compact encoders for net | `FF.u` |\n| `02-tuning.js` | every tunable with its default, range and group (§22), registered as `Dev …` settings (E7); `FF.T.get(key)` | `FF.T` |\n| `10-data-teams.js` | 6 teams: colors, logos, rosters, stats, looks, mascots, coaches, AI personalities, stars and specials (§18.1) | `FF.data.teams` |\n| `11-data-plays.js` | formations, offensive, defensive and special plays, kickoff and PAT choices (§18.3) | `FF.data.formations`, `.plays`, `.dplays` |\n| `12-data-stadiums.js` | 5 stadiums (§18.2) | `FF.data.stadiums` |\n| `13-data-cards.js` | 8 Fiasco Cards with their effects as bus listeners (§10.2) | `FF.data.cards` |\n| `14-data-story.js` | chapters, cutscene scripts (E.Story nodes), halftime choices, rewards, loss lines (§15.1, §18.6) | `FF.data.story` |\n| `15-data-drills.js` | the tutorial and practice drills (§15.2) | `FF.data.drills` |\n| `16-data-lines.js` | commentary line banks per event, refs, QB cadences, crowd chants (§18.5) | `FF.data.lines` |\n| `17-data-attract.js` | the attract director's segments (§15.4) | `FF.data.attract` |\n| `20-audio-voice.js` | the babble voice synth and the speaker profiles (§13.4) | `FF.voice` |\n| `21-audio-music.js` | songs, layers, stings, the sfx definitions, the crowd bed (§13) | `FF.music`, `FF.sfx`, `FF.crowdAudio` |\n| `22-glyphs.js` | button chips per device and glyph setting; prompt strings (§9.3) | `FF.glyph` |\n| `23-ui-kit.js` | 10-foot canvas UI: a focus grid with mouse hover, big buttons, cards, tabs, panels, comic bursts, the prompt strip, captions, toasts | `FF.ui` |\n| `24-shapes.js` | the game's own `E.SHAPES` entries (§12.6) and its materials | — |\n| `25-field.js` | the stadium builder: TileMap, floor painting, stands, goalposts, pylons, markers, sideline dressing, lights, weather (§5) | `FF.Stadium` |\n| `26-crowd.js` | crowd agents, excitement, waves, signs, flashes (§11.4) | `FF.Crowd` |\n| `27-rigs.js` | athlete and cast rig factory, the gear hook (§12.2), pose codes to rig drive (§12.3), the mocap player, PoseFX | `FF.Rigs` |\n| `28-ball.js` | the football: held, thrown, kicked, loose; drawing the spiral, shadow and trails (§7.6) | `FF.Ball` |\n| `30-sim.js` | the match: the state machine, rules, clock, possession, spotting (§6) | `FF.sim` |\n| `31-sim-athletes.js` | athletes, movement, turbo, collisions, blocking contact (§7.1–7.4, §7.9) | |\n| `32-sim-actions.js` | actions and their resolution: throw, catch, tackle, Big Hit, dive, spin, stiff arm, hurdle, strip, lateral, handoff, kick, late hit, pile-up (§7.5–7.8) | |\n| `33-ai.js` | athlete brains and behaviors (§8.1–8.4) | `FF.ai` |\n| `34-ai-coach.js` | CPU play calling, cards, 4th down, clock, difficulty, COACH SAYS (§8.5) | `FF.coach` |\n| `35-control.js` | human control: assignment, switching, intents from devices, the mouse pointer on the field (§9) | `FF.control` |\n| `36-ots.js` | the over-the-top systems: Hot Streak, Fiasco Meter, cards in play, boss specials, Dinged, helmets popping off, mascots, refs, lights-out (§10) | `FF.ots` |\n| `37-camera.js` | the camera director and views (§11.1) | `FF.cam` |\n| `38-replay.js` | the recorder, replay player and highlight reel (§11.2) | `FF.replay` |\n| `39-commentary.js` | the announcer and on-field text pops (§11.3) | `FF.talk` |\n| `40-fx.js` | hit FX, divots, confetti, fireworks, pyro, breath puffs, fire trails (§11.5) | `FF.fx` |\n| `41-hud.js` | score bug, turbo, meters, prompts, captions, event text, receiver chips, kick meter (§14.6) | `FF.hud` |\n| `50-scene-title.js` | title, attract trigger, touch-only gate | scene `title` |\n| `51-scene-menus.js` | main menu, exhibition setup, controller assignment, team and stadium select, Hall of Fiasco, About/Credits | scenes `menu`, `setup`, `records`, `about` |\n| `52-scene-match.js` | the match scene, which ties sim, control, AI, camera, HUD, play call, kicking, halftime, results and pause items together | scene `match` |\n| `53-playcall.js` | the play-call UI (§14.5) | `FF.PlayCall` |\n| `54-scene-story.js` | the story hub (the bracket board), the cutscene player, chapter intros and outros, rewards, ending and credits | scenes `story`, `cutscene`, `credits` |\n| `55-scene-drills.js` | tutorial and practice drills | scene `drill` |\n| `56-scene-attract.js` | the attract director and its overlay | scene `attract` |\n| `57-scene-hubcard.js` | the AI Hub card and countdown (§16.3) | scene `hubcard` |\n| `70-dev.js` | Dev mode: screens, sliders, toggles, situations, Play Lab, overlays (§17) | `FF.dev` |\n| `80-net.js` | the AI Hub adapter: roles, messages, snapshots, prediction, loopback for tests (§16) | `FF.net` |\n| `90-main.js` | registers the scenes, the boot steps, deep links (`#match`, `#drill/passing`, `#attract`, `#story/ch3`), `game.debug`, `game.start` | |\n\n### 4.4 Scenes and the flow between them\n```\nboot ─▶ (AIHub.active?) ─yes─▶ hubcard ─▶ match(session) ─▶ (hub ends the round)\n  └─no─▶ title ─(10 s idle)─▶ attract ─(any input)─▶ title\n           └─▶ menu ─▶ story ─▶ cutscene ⇄ match / drill ─▶ credits\n                    ├▶ setup (exhibition) ─▶ match ─▶ results (inside match) ─▶ menu\n                    ├▶ drill (practice)   ├▶ records   ├▶ about   └▶ Options (game.menu('settings'))\n```\nGameplay scenes (`match`, `drill`) are `pausable: true`, except in hub sessions (§16.6). Their pause menu shows Resume,\n**Dev Mode**, **Restart** (exhibition and practice only), **Quit to Menu**, then the standard Settings, Controls, Full\nscreen and Help. Titles, menus and cutscenes aren't pausable, but the corner menu button opens Settings there.\n\n### 4.5 One step of a match (`match.update(dt)`)\n1. `FF.control.update(dt)`: device input to intents for human-held athletes, including mouse picks.\n2. `FF.ai.update(dt)`: AI brains think at 20 Hz, staggered by athlete index, and emit intents every step from their\n   current behavior.\n3. In a hub session on the host, `FF.net.applyRemote()` writes remote players' intents and predicted positions.\n4. `FF.sim.step(dt, intents)`: phase timers, athlete movement and actions, collisions and blocks, the ball, contacts\n   (tackles, catches, recoveries), boundaries, rules and the clock. It emits events.\n5. `FF.ots.update(dt)`, then `FF.replay.record()`.\n6. Presentation, skipped headless when `FF.fast` is set: `FF.Rigs.update(dt)`, `FF.cam.update(dt)`,\n   `FF.Crowd.update(dt)`, `FF.talk.update(dt)`, `FF.hud.update(dt)`.\n7. In a hub session, `FF.net.send()` on its own clock (§16.5).\n\n`draw(r)`: stadium floor and walls, decals (field lines of scrimmage, the pass target marker, divots), shapes and\nprops, the crowd, athletes (`r.actor`, with `{ pick: athleteId }` for the mouse), the ball, refs and sideline cast, FX,\nlights, the HUD in `r.overlay`, the play-call UI, then captions.\n\n### 4.6 The `game.debug` handle (for tests and tools)\n```js\ngame.debug = {\n  snapshot(),            // { scene, mode, phase, clock, quarter, score, down, toGo, los, possession, ball: {state, x, y, z, holder},\n                         //   athletes: [{ id, team, pos, x, y, z, pose, ctl }], controlled: { p1: id, ... }, events: last 10, errors }\n  scenario(spec),        // load a scenario (A.5) now\n  cheat(name, on),       // godMode, infiniteTurbo, forceCatch, forceFumble, forceINT, noTackle, cpuVsCpu, ...\n  playLab(off, def, n),  // run n headless plays of off vs def, return stats (§8.6)\n  skipCutscene(), finishGame(winnerSide), setClock(s), setScore(h, a), giveCard(side, id), setHot(athleteId),\n  fast(on),              // presentation off for speed (headless only)\n};\n```\n`tools/check.mjs` prints `snapshot()` after its run.\n\n---\n\n## 5. World, units and the stadium\n\n### 5.1 Units\n**One yard is one tile, 16 world units.** The engine's own scale is 16 units = 1 m and a 26-unit hero, so players","numLines":215,"startLine":120,"totalLines":2454}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01DNDHoPEK7nP6hodfEuSRrn","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
