{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh3eceP8fWPS1o2kzKPD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GAsw5FD7ht5hmBmWLuzgTC","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174831__x898ed5/work/PLAN.md","offset":350,"limit":150},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1065,"cache_read_input_tokens":560461,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1065},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c28e7abf-db6e-401a-a911-43ba9983a968","uuid":"9cef6c28-ad0e-4dec-b8cc-0b809fc65220","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:48:33.124Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh3ecLHDB4wUMxn5GF2x","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01GAsw5FD7ht5hmBmWLuzgTC":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174831__x898ed5/work/PLAN.md","offset":350,"limit":150}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GAsw5FD7ht5hmBmWLuzgTC","type":"tool_result","content":"350\t## 5. World, units and the stadium\n351\t\n352\t### 5.1 Units\n353\t**One yard is one tile, 16 world units.** The engine's own scale is 16 units = 1 m and a 26-unit hero, so players\n354\t(about 1.9–2.2 yd tall at rig sizes 1.2–1.45) and the store's props stay in proportion. z is always up. Angles are\n355\tradians, 0 east. `FF.u.yd(n) = n * 16`.\n356\t\n357\t### 5.2 Field geometry\n358\t- The playing field is 100 yards between the goal lines, plus 10-yard end zones, so 120 × 53 yards.\n359\t- The TileMap is 128 columns × 70 rows. Columns 0–3 are outside the left end, 4–123 are the 120 field yards (end\n360\t  zones at 4–13 and 114–123), and 124–127 are outside the right end.\n361\t- Rows from the top: 0–7 are the far stands (8 tiers), 8–11 the far sideline area, 12–64 the 53 field rows, and 65–69\n362\t  the near sideline (benches, cheerleaders, photographers, chain gang).\n363\t- World constants (in `25-field.js`): `END_L = 64` (the left end line, x), `GOAL_L = 224`, `GOAL_R = 1824`,\n364\t  `END_R = 1984`, `SIDE_T = 192` (the far sideline, y), `SIDE_B = 1040` (the near sideline, y), `MID_Y = 616`.\n365\t- Yard lines run every 5 yards. Numbers every 10 sit 12 yards in from each sideline. Hash marks every yard sit at\n366\t  `MID_Y ± 3 yd` (arcade hashes, close to the middle). A team abbreviation is painted in each end zone, and the home\n367\t  logo at midfield.\n368\t- The ball spot after a play: x where the ball was when it died, y clamped to the hashes.\n369\t- A team attacking right has `dir = +1`; attacking left, `dir = -1`. Field position \"own 20\" for a team attacking right\n370\t  is x = `GOAL_L + 20*16`. `FF.sim.yardline(x, side)` converts both ways.\n371\t- Goalposts sit at each end line (x = END_L, END_R; y = MID_Y): a base post, a gooseneck 1 yd toward the field, the\n372\t  crossbar at **53** units (10 ft), and uprights **99** units apart (18.5 ft) rising **160** above the bar. Draw them\n373\t  with `r.queue` lines like the SPORTS kit's posts, 2 px wide, in yellow `#ffd84a` with a `#c89a1a` shaded side. They\n374\t  are drawn, not shapes, because they need exact proportions and a hit test for kicks.\n375\t- Pylons sit at the 4 goal-line and 4 end-line corners (shape `pylon`, §12.6).\n376\t\n377\t### 5.3 The TileMap\n378\t- Legend: `.` grass (floor tag `grass`), `e` end zone (`endzone`), `s` sideline (`sideline`), `m` mud (`mud`), `i` ice\n379\t  (`ice`), `,` outside (`outside`), and `1`–`8` the stand tiers (wall types 11–18).\n380\t- Wall types 11–18: heights 14, 26, 38, 50, 62, 74, 86, 98, each 1 tile deep, with\n381\t  `cut: true, cutH: 8` so reversed and rotated views see past them. Faces and roofs come from the stadium\n382\t  (`face: 'plank'` bleachers at Tumbleweed, `'stone'` concrete at the Mega Dome, `roof: 'slab'`).\n383\t- Behind the top tier is a back wall (type 19, h 120) with banners (props) and, by stadium, a scoreboard or jumbotron\n384\t  shape.\n385\t- Side stands behind each end zone (columns 0–3) are 4 tiers (types 11–14), so an end zone view has a crowd too.\n386\t- `outside`: the stadium's ground color beyond the map. The sky comes from an `E.Backdrop` drawn first in `draw`\n387\t  (mesas, bayou trees, a skyline, mountains with the northern lights, the dome's ceiling truss). It shows above the\n388\t  far stands when the camera looks high.\n389\t\n390\t### 5.4 Floor painting (`floorTex(x, y, tag)`)\n391\t- Grass in mown stripes 5 yards wide, alternating two greens per stadium, with per-pixel noise (`E.hash2`,\n392\t  `E.noise2`), like the SPORTS kit's floor.\n393\t- White paint for yard lines (a band 1.2 units wide), hashes (0.8 yd long), and the sidelines and end lines (2 units).\n394\t  Numbers are the 5×7 font's digit bitmaps scaled to 2.5 yd tall, sampled per world pixel. End-zone letters and the\n395\t  midfield logo work the same way from their bitmaps (§12.8).\n396\t- Mud (`mud` tag and noise blotches) is brown and wet-dark. Ice is pale blue with streaks. Divots are decals added at\n397\t  runtime (§11.5).\n398\t- Night stadiums: `[r, g, b, glow]` texels make the Afterglow Arena's yard lines glow. A wear pass darkens the grass\n399\t  between the hashes.\n400\t- The floor bakes once per view (engine behavior). View changes (Kick Cam, replays) rebake. That's fine: cut, don't\n401\t  rotate continuously (§11.1).\n402\t\n403\t### 5.5 Dressing per stadium\n404\tEach stadium (§18.2) lists its props: shapes and `r.prop`s on the far sideline (benches, coolers, the Gatorade-style\n405\tcooler table, TV cameras, light towers at the 4 corners), the near sideline (team benches, the cheerleader squad, the\n406\tmascot, the chain gang with down markers that move to the line of scrimmage and the line to gain, photographers),\n407\tbehind the end zones (pyro cannons, banners, a parked pickup or an airboat), and the backdrop layers. Aim for 3–8\n408\tprops on any screen (the API's look target). Light towers carry `lights: [...]` that light the field at night.\n409\t\n410\t### 5.6 Views\n411\t| Id | Made as | Used for |\n412\t|---|---|---|\n413\t| `broadcast` | `new E.View('broadcast', 'Broadcast', 0, 50, 1, 1)` | the default for play |\n414\t| `sky` | `new E.View('sky', 'Sky Cam', 0, 65, 1, 1)` | option (V cycles; Settings, Camera) |\n415\t| `blimp` | `E.VIEWS.topdown` | option |\n416\t| `kickcam` | `new E.View('kickcam', 'Kick Cam', ±90, 32, 1, 1)`, turned so the kick goes up the screen | FGs and PATs (and kickoffs from behind the kicker for 1.2 s) |\n417\t| `field` | `new E.View('field', 'Field Level', 0, 25, 1, 1)` (the brawler pitch) | replays, celebrations, cutscenes on the field |\n418\t| `iso` | `E.VIEWS.iso` | replays |\n419\t| `reverse` | `new E.View('reverse', 'Reverse Angle', 180, 45, 1, 1)` | replays (the far stands cut low) |\n420\t\n421\tSteer with `game.view.screenDirToGround(mx, my)`, so pushing up always means up the screen in every view, including\n422\tthe Kick Cam. `E.charView` draws rigs from a lower angle in pitch 40–89 views automatically, and that's wanted.\n423\t\n424\t---\n425\t\n426\t## 6. Football rules and the match flow (`30-sim.js`)\n427\t\n428\t### 6.1 The match state machine\n429\t```\n430\tCOIN_TOSS ─▶ KICKOFF_SETUP ─▶ KICK_LIVE ─▶ (DEAD) ─▶ PLAYCALL ─▶ PRESNAP ─▶ LIVE ─▶ DEAD ─▶ ...\n431\tDEAD branches: score ─▶ CELEBRATE ─▶ [REPLAY] ─▶ PAT_CALL ─▶ PAT ─▶ KICKOFF_SETUP\n432\t               turnover / downs ─▶ PLAYCALL (other side)    quarter end ─▶ QUARTER_BREAK\n433\t               halftime ─▶ HALFTIME ─▶ KICKOFF_SETUP        end of regulation, tied ─▶ OVERTIME ─▶ ...\n434\t               final ─▶ FINAL (results)\n435\t```\n436\t- **COIN_TOSS** (1.5 s): the home mascot flips a giant coin (cosmetic). The winner is `FF.rng.sim`'s, or the\n437\t  scenario's. The winner receives.\n438\t- **PLAYCALL**: both sides choose on the play-call UI (§14.5). The CPU chooses after a human-like 0.6–1.4 s delay.\n439\t  The timer is `T.playcallTime` (12 s). When it runs out, the COACH SAYS play is picked. Kicking situations show the\n440\t  special cards.\n441\t- **PRESNAP**: athletes jog to their formation spots (1.2 s), then the line sets. The offense hikes with b1. The CPU\n442\t  hikes after 0.5–2.0 s. A human gets an auto-hike when `T.autoHike` (8 s) runs out, with a visible play clock from\n443\t  5 s. The defense's human may move their defender behind the line. The QB barks his cadence (§13.4).\n444\t- **LIVE**: the play. It ends with a **dead ball**: a carrier down (tackled, or a knee or body touching the ground\n445\t  after a dive), out of bounds, an incomplete pass, a score, a pile-up resolved, a kick dead (touchback, out of\n446\t  bounds, downed), or a kneel.\n447\t- **DEAD** (1.2 s, or longer for presentation): refs signal, the chain gang moves, late hits are possible for\n448\t  `T.lateHitWindow` (1.2 s), then the spot and downs update.\n449\t- **CELEBRATE** (after a TD, 3.5 s, skippable with b1): the scorer's dance (§10.6), pyro, the crowd, the announcer.\n450\t  Then **REPLAY** if the Replays setting allows (§11.2).\n451\t- **PAT_CALL**: KICK XP (1 point, the snap from the 15) or GO FOR 2 (a normal play from the 3).\n452\t- **QUARTER_BREAK** (1.5 s, a card \"END OF THE 1ST\"). Teams switch ends only at halftime.\n453\t- **HALFTIME**: story mode plays the locker-room cutscene with an adjustment choice. Exhibition shows the halftime\n454\t  show (cheerleaders, band, mascot; 6 s, skippable) and 4 stat tiles. A hub session gets a 3 s card.\n455\t- **OVERTIME**: tied after Q4. A coin toss, then sudden death: the first score wins, with a period of\n456\t  `T.quarterLength`. Still tied after it: exhibition ends in a TIE; story chapters say \"That's a FIASCO!\" and offer a\n457\t  rematch; a hub round finishes tied (every player in one standings array).\n458\t- **FINAL**: the whistle, the winners cheer (`pose: 'cheer'`) and the losers kneel. Then the results screen (§14.8).\n459\t\n460\t### 6.2 Downs, distance and spotting\n461\t- First and **20** (`T.yardsToGo`, 10–30). Gain the distance within 4 downs for a new set. Otherwise it's a turnover\n462\t  on downs at the spot.\n463\t- Forward progress is the ball's x when the play dies. A dive gains to where the ball lands.\n464\t- An incomplete pass puts the ball back at the previous spot.\n465\t- A loss behind the line counts against the distance. \"2ND & 27\" is legal and funny.\n466\t- Goal to go: when the line to gain is past the goal line, show \"& GOAL\".\n467\t- The first-down line (yellow) and the line of scrimmage (blue) are drawn on the field as decals, broadcast style.\n468\t\n469\t### 6.3 Scoring\n470\t| Score | Points | After |\n471\t|---|---|---|\n472\t| Touchdown: the ball carried over the goal line (the ball's x), or caught in the end zone with both feet in (simplified: the catch point inside it) | 6 | CELEBRATE, then PAT_CALL |\n473\t| Extra point kick | 1 | kickoff |\n474\t| Two-point conversion (any play from the 3) | 2 | kickoff |\n475\t| Field goal (between the uprights and over the bar, §7.8) | 3 | kickoff by the scoring team |\n476\t| Safety (the carrier downed in his own end zone) | 2 | the team that gave up the safety kicks off from its own 20 |\n477\t| Defensive TD on an INT or fumble return | 6 | as a touchdown |\n478\t\n479\t### 6.4 Kicks\n480\t- **Kickoff** from the kicking team's 30. Choices: DEEP (meter power 0.6–1.0 gives 50–70 yd), SQUIB (a low bouncer of\n481\t  30–40 yd that is hard to field), ONSIDE (10–14 yd, high bounce; recovery is a scramble, §7.7).\n482\t- **Receiving:** the returner auto-catches when under the ball within 1.2 yd. A ball caught in the end zone becomes a\n483\t  **touchback** at the 25 if the returner presses b1 (take a knee) or stays in the end zone for 1.5 s. A kick out of\n484\t  bounds puts the ball at the receiving team's 40.\n485\t- **Punt:** a long snap to the punter 12 yd deep, then the meter. A return, or a fair catch: the returner presses b1\n486\t  before the catch. A ball into the end zone is a touchback at the 20.\n487\t- **FG / PAT:** a snap to the holder 7 yd deep, a 0.35 s plant, the kick. The Kick Cam (§11.1). Rushers can block a\n488\t  kick if one reaches within 1.2 yd of the kick point before contact (`T.blockChance` 0.6).\n489\t\n490\t### 6.5 The clock\n491\t- Quarters of `T.quarterLength`: 2:00 in exhibition (a setting: 1:00, 1:30, 2:00, 3:00, 5:00), set per chapter in\n492\t  story mode, 1:30 in hub rounds.\n493\t- The clock runs during live plays. After an in-bounds play that wasn't a score, an incompletion or a turnover, it\n494\t  runs `T.runoff` (10 s) more (the huddle), but not in the last 30 s of a half.\n495\t- It stops for the play-call screen and every presentation beat. The play-call timer is separate (12 s).\n496\t- Hurry-up: in the last minute of a half the music tempo rises 8% (`audio.tempo`) and the announcer counts down.\n497\t- The clock never goes below 0. A play that starts at 0:01 is played out. At 0:00 the quarter ends when the ball is\n498\t  dead.\n499\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c28e7abf-db6e-401a-a911-43ba9983a968","uuid":"0ccc4a7f-732a-4f9d-a4c1-dc8151c0e4e6","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:48:33.134Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174831__x898ed5/work/PLAN.md","content":"## 5. World, units and the stadium\n\n### 5.1 Units\n**One yard is one tile, 16 world units.** The engine's own scale is 16 units = 1 m and a 26-unit hero, so players\n(about 1.9–2.2 yd tall at rig sizes 1.2–1.45) and the store's props stay in proportion. z is always up. Angles are\nradians, 0 east. `FF.u.yd(n) = n * 16`.\n\n### 5.2 Field geometry\n- The playing field is 100 yards between the goal lines, plus 10-yard end zones, so 120 × 53 yards.\n- The TileMap is 128 columns × 70 rows. Columns 0–3 are outside the left end, 4–123 are the 120 field yards (end\n  zones at 4–13 and 114–123), and 124–127 are outside the right end.\n- Rows from the top: 0–7 are the far stands (8 tiers), 8–11 the far sideline area, 12–64 the 53 field rows, and 65–69\n  the near sideline (benches, cheerleaders, photographers, chain gang).\n- World constants (in `25-field.js`): `END_L = 64` (the left end line, x), `GOAL_L = 224`, `GOAL_R = 1824`,\n  `END_R = 1984`, `SIDE_T = 192` (the far sideline, y), `SIDE_B = 1040` (the near sideline, y), `MID_Y = 616`.\n- Yard lines run every 5 yards. Numbers every 10 sit 12 yards in from each sideline. Hash marks every yard sit at\n  `MID_Y ± 3 yd` (arcade hashes, close to the middle). A team abbreviation is painted in each end zone, and the home\n  logo at midfield.\n- The ball spot after a play: x where the ball was when it died, y clamped to the hashes.\n- A team attacking right has `dir = +1`; attacking left, `dir = -1`. Field position \"own 20\" for a team attacking right\n  is x = `GOAL_L + 20*16`. `FF.sim.yardline(x, side)` converts both ways.\n- Goalposts sit at each end line (x = END_L, END_R; y = MID_Y): a base post, a gooseneck 1 yd toward the field, the\n  crossbar at **53** units (10 ft), and uprights **99** units apart (18.5 ft) rising **160** above the bar. Draw them\n  with `r.queue` lines like the SPORTS kit's posts, 2 px wide, in yellow `#ffd84a` with a `#c89a1a` shaded side. They\n  are drawn, not shapes, because they need exact proportions and a hit test for kicks.\n- Pylons sit at the 4 goal-line and 4 end-line corners (shape `pylon`, §12.6).\n\n### 5.3 The TileMap\n- Legend: `.` grass (floor tag `grass`), `e` end zone (`endzone`), `s` sideline (`sideline`), `m` mud (`mud`), `i` ice\n  (`ice`), `,` outside (`outside`), and `1`–`8` the stand tiers (wall types 11–18).\n- Wall types 11–18: heights 14, 26, 38, 50, 62, 74, 86, 98, each 1 tile deep, with\n  `cut: true, cutH: 8` so reversed and rotated views see past them. Faces and roofs come from the stadium\n  (`face: 'plank'` bleachers at Tumbleweed, `'stone'` concrete at the Mega Dome, `roof: 'slab'`).\n- Behind the top tier is a back wall (type 19, h 120) with banners (props) and, by stadium, a scoreboard or jumbotron\n  shape.\n- Side stands behind each end zone (columns 0–3) are 4 tiers (types 11–14), so an end zone view has a crowd too.\n- `outside`: the stadium's ground color beyond the map. The sky comes from an `E.Backdrop` drawn first in `draw`\n  (mesas, bayou trees, a skyline, mountains with the northern lights, the dome's ceiling truss). It shows above the\n  far stands when the camera looks high.\n\n### 5.4 Floor painting (`floorTex(x, y, tag)`)\n- Grass in mown stripes 5 yards wide, alternating two greens per stadium, with per-pixel noise (`E.hash2`,\n  `E.noise2`), like the SPORTS kit's floor.\n- White paint for yard lines (a band 1.2 units wide), hashes (0.8 yd long), and the sidelines and end lines (2 units).\n  Numbers are the 5×7 font's digit bitmaps scaled to 2.5 yd tall, sampled per world pixel. End-zone letters and the\n  midfield logo work the same way from their bitmaps (§12.8).\n- Mud (`mud` tag and noise blotches) is brown and wet-dark. Ice is pale blue with streaks. Divots are decals added at\n  runtime (§11.5).\n- Night stadiums: `[r, g, b, glow]` texels make the Afterglow Arena's yard lines glow. A wear pass darkens the grass\n  between the hashes.\n- The floor bakes once per view (engine behavior). View changes (Kick Cam, replays) rebake. That's fine: cut, don't\n  rotate continuously (§11.1).\n\n### 5.5 Dressing per stadium\nEach stadium (§18.2) lists its props: shapes and `r.prop`s on the far sideline (benches, coolers, the Gatorade-style\ncooler table, TV cameras, light towers at the 4 corners), the near sideline (team benches, the cheerleader squad, the\nmascot, the chain gang with down markers that move to the line of scrimmage and the line to gain, photographers),\nbehind the end zones (pyro cannons, banners, a parked pickup or an airboat), and the backdrop layers. Aim for 3–8\nprops on any screen (the API's look target). Light towers carry `lights: [...]` that light the field at night.\n\n### 5.6 Views\n| Id | Made as | Used for |\n|---|---|---|\n| `broadcast` | `new E.View('broadcast', 'Broadcast', 0, 50, 1, 1)` | the default for play |\n| `sky` | `new E.View('sky', 'Sky Cam', 0, 65, 1, 1)` | option (V cycles; Settings, Camera) |\n| `blimp` | `E.VIEWS.topdown` | option |\n| `kickcam` | `new E.View('kickcam', 'Kick Cam', ±90, 32, 1, 1)`, turned so the kick goes up the screen | FGs and PATs (and kickoffs from behind the kicker for 1.2 s) |\n| `field` | `new E.View('field', 'Field Level', 0, 25, 1, 1)` (the brawler pitch) | replays, celebrations, cutscenes on the field |\n| `iso` | `E.VIEWS.iso` | replays |\n| `reverse` | `new E.View('reverse', 'Reverse Angle', 180, 45, 1, 1)` | replays (the far stands cut low) |\n\nSteer with `game.view.screenDirToGround(mx, my)`, so pushing up always means up the screen in every view, including\nthe Kick Cam. `E.charView` draws rigs from a lower angle in pitch 40–89 views automatically, and that's wanted.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Football rules and the match flow (`30-sim.js`)\n\n### 6.1 The match state machine\n```\nCOIN_TOSS ─▶ KICKOFF_SETUP ─▶ KICK_LIVE ─▶ (DEAD) ─▶ PLAYCALL ─▶ PRESNAP ─▶ LIVE ─▶ DEAD ─▶ ...\nDEAD branches: score ─▶ CELEBRATE ─▶ [REPLAY] ─▶ PAT_CALL ─▶ PAT ─▶ KICKOFF_SETUP\n               turnover / downs ─▶ PLAYCALL (other side)    quarter end ─▶ QUARTER_BREAK\n               halftime ─▶ HALFTIME ─▶ KICKOFF_SETUP        end of regulation, tied ─▶ OVERTIME ─▶ ...\n               final ─▶ FINAL (results)\n```\n- **COIN_TOSS** (1.5 s): the home mascot flips a giant coin (cosmetic). The winner is `FF.rng.sim`'s, or the\n  scenario's. The winner receives.\n- **PLAYCALL**: both sides choose on the play-call UI (§14.5). The CPU chooses after a human-like 0.6–1.4 s delay.\n  The timer is `T.playcallTime` (12 s). When it runs out, the COACH SAYS play is picked. Kicking situations show the\n  special cards.\n- **PRESNAP**: athletes jog to their formation spots (1.2 s), then the line sets. The offense hikes with b1. The CPU\n  hikes after 0.5–2.0 s. A human gets an auto-hike when `T.autoHike` (8 s) runs out, with a visible play clock from\n  5 s. The defense's human may move their defender behind the line. The QB barks his cadence (§13.4).\n- **LIVE**: the play. It ends with a **dead ball**: a carrier down (tackled, or a knee or body touching the ground\n  after a dive), out of bounds, an incomplete pass, a score, a pile-up resolved, a kick dead (touchback, out of\n  bounds, downed), or a kneel.\n- **DEAD** (1.2 s, or longer for presentation): refs signal, the chain gang moves, late hits are possible for\n  `T.lateHitWindow` (1.2 s), then the spot and downs update.\n- **CELEBRATE** (after a TD, 3.5 s, skippable with b1): the scorer's dance (§10.6), pyro, the crowd, the announcer.\n  Then **REPLAY** if the Replays setting allows (§11.2).\n- **PAT_CALL**: KICK XP (1 point, the snap from the 15) or GO FOR 2 (a normal play from the 3).\n- **QUARTER_BREAK** (1.5 s, a card \"END OF THE 1ST\"). Teams switch ends only at halftime.\n- **HALFTIME**: story mode plays the locker-room cutscene with an adjustment choice. Exhibition shows the halftime\n  show (cheerleaders, band, mascot; 6 s, skippable) and 4 stat tiles. A hub session gets a 3 s card.\n- **OVERTIME**: tied after Q4. A coin toss, then sudden death: the first score wins, with a period of\n  `T.quarterLength`. Still tied after it: exhibition ends in a TIE; story chapters say \"That's a FIASCO!\" and offer a\n  rematch; a hub round finishes tied (every player in one standings array).\n- **FINAL**: the whistle, the winners cheer (`pose: 'cheer'`) and the losers kneel. Then the results screen (§14.8).\n\n### 6.2 Downs, distance and spotting\n- First and **20** (`T.yardsToGo`, 10–30). Gain the distance within 4 downs for a new set. Otherwise it's a turnover\n  on downs at the spot.\n- Forward progress is the ball's x when the play dies. A dive gains to where the ball lands.\n- An incomplete pass puts the ball back at the previous spot.\n- A loss behind the line counts against the distance. \"2ND & 27\" is legal and funny.\n- Goal to go: when the line to gain is past the goal line, show \"& GOAL\".\n- The first-down line (yellow) and the line of scrimmage (blue) are drawn on the field as decals, broadcast style.\n\n### 6.3 Scoring\n| Score | Points | After |\n|---|---|---|\n| Touchdown: the ball carried over the goal line (the ball's x), or caught in the end zone with both feet in (simplified: the catch point inside it) | 6 | CELEBRATE, then PAT_CALL |\n| Extra point kick | 1 | kickoff |\n| Two-point conversion (any play from the 3) | 2 | kickoff |\n| Field goal (between the uprights and over the bar, §7.8) | 3 | kickoff by the scoring team |\n| Safety (the carrier downed in his own end zone) | 2 | the team that gave up the safety kicks off from its own 20 |\n| Defensive TD on an INT or fumble return | 6 | as a touchdown |\n\n### 6.4 Kicks\n- **Kickoff** from the kicking team's 30. Choices: DEEP (meter power 0.6–1.0 gives 50–70 yd), SQUIB (a low bouncer of\n  30–40 yd that is hard to field), ONSIDE (10–14 yd, high bounce; recovery is a scramble, §7.7).\n- **Receiving:** the returner auto-catches when under the ball within 1.2 yd. A ball caught in the end zone becomes a\n  **touchback** at the 25 if the returner presses b1 (take a knee) or stays in the end zone for 1.5 s. A kick out of\n  bounds puts the ball at the receiving team's 40.\n- **Punt:** a long snap to the punter 12 yd deep, then the meter. A return, or a fair catch: the returner presses b1\n  before the catch. A ball into the end zone is a touchback at the 20.\n- **FG / PAT:** a snap to the holder 7 yd deep, a 0.35 s plant, the kick. The Kick Cam (§11.1). Rushers can block a\n  kick if one reaches within 1.2 yd of the kick point before contact (`T.blockChance` 0.6).\n\n### 6.5 The clock\n- Quarters of `T.quarterLength`: 2:00 in exhibition (a setting: 1:00, 1:30, 2:00, 3:00, 5:00), set per chapter in\n  story mode, 1:30 in hub rounds.\n- The clock runs during live plays. After an in-bounds play that wasn't a score, an incompletion or a turnover, it\n  runs `T.runoff` (10 s) more (the huddle), but not in the last 30 s of a half.\n- It stops for the play-call screen and every presentation beat. The play-call timer is separate (12 s).\n- Hurry-up: in the last minute of a half the music tempo rises 8% (`audio.tempo`) and the announcer counts down.\n- The clock never goes below 0. A play that starts at 0:01 is played out. At 0:00 the quarter ends when the ball is\n  dead.\n","numLines":150,"startLine":350,"totalLines":2474}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01GAsw5FD7ht5hmBmWLuzgTC","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
