{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh49W1LLwrm9uom6fm1L","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QhQc8pTqkaBkqPRwprURP7","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174831__x898ed5/work/PLAN.md","offset":1310,"limit":100},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":466,"cache_read_input_tokens":618236,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":466},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c28e7abf-db6e-401a-a911-43ba9983a968","uuid":"58b07567-559e-4ae4-8204-51ecd277b6c0","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:55:10.122Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh49Vhj3TPmCeEhhHhfj","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01QhQc8pTqkaBkqPRwprURP7":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174831__x898ed5/work/PLAN.md","offset":1310,"limit":100}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QhQc8pTqkaBkqPRwprURP7","type":"tool_result","content":"1310\t---\n1311\t\n1312\t## 13. Audio (`20-audio-voice.js`, `21-audio-music.js`)\n1313\tAll sound is synthesized: the engine's chip synth (`audio.sfx`, `audio.music`, `audio.define`) plus our Web Audio\n1314\tnodes on engine buses (E6). There are no audio files. Every composition is original: write new melodies, and\n1315\tdon't transcribe known stadium tunes or fight songs.\n1316\t\n1317\t### 13.1 Music (`{ bpm, steps, tracks }`, named tracks for layers)\n1318\t| Song | For | Character |\n1319\t|---|---|---|\n1320\t| FIASCO ANTHEM | the title, the story hub | stadium rock, 132 bpm: a square lead, saw bass, a `k . s .` beat, a cheer-like hook |\n1321\t| LOCKER ROOM FUNK | menus, setup | 110 bpm, a slap-bass `pulse12` line, offbeat hats |\n1322\t| HUDDLE UP | the play-call screen | a drumline loop (drums only plus a low triangle ostinato), 120 bpm, 2 bars, rising tension |\n1323\t| DUST DEVIL STOMP | Tumbleweed Field | a western shuffle, triangle bass, a twangy pulse lead, 128 bpm |\n1324\t| SWAMP STOMP | The Hog Wallow | a blues shuffle, saw bass, a harmonica-like pulse with vibrato, 96 bpm |\n1325\t| NEON NIGHTDRIVE | Afterglow Arena | synthwave, saw arpeggios, a square lead, 118 bpm |\n1326\t| FROSTBITE FANFARE | Frostbite Field | bells (triangle and sine arpeggios), 100 bpm |\n1327\t| MEGA DOME ANTHEM | the Mega Dome | epic brass (3 squares in fifths), 140 bpm |\n1328\t| HALFTIME SHOW | halftime | a marching band: `k s s` drums, a brass melody |\n1329\t| ARCADE ATTRACT | the attract mode | a 150 bpm remix of the anthem |\n1330\t| FIASCO FOREVER | the credits | a slow ballad version of the anthem |\n1331\t| Stings | `audio.sting` | TD fanfare, FG good, first-down organ riff, defense chant riff, Fiasco Break \"wah-wah\", victory jingle, defeat jingle (a sad sliding saw) |\n1332\t\n1333\tIn matches the stadium song plays low under the crowd bed, with layers `hot` (more drums and a guitar-like saw, for a\n1334\tBlazing athlete), `redzone` (bass pulses inside the 20) and `final` (`audio.tempo` +8% in the last minute). Between\n1335\tplays, original stadium-organ riffs (square and triangle chords with vibrato) play as stings on first downs, and a\n1336\tdefense-chant riff plays on 3rd down.\n1337\t\n1338\t### 13.2 Sound effects (`audio.define` each; the engine warns on unknown names, and a test fails on such a warning)\n1339\thike (the QB's \"HUT\" voice + a snap thump), whistle, helmetClang (square 1800→1200 + a triangle ring), padsCrunch\n1340\t(lowpass noise 900→200 + sine 120→50), bigHit (crunch + clang + a boom sine 80→30), tackleThud, dive (whoosh),\n1341\tspinWhoosh, stiffArm (punch), hurdle (jump2), catchSlap (bandpass noise 1200, short), drop (thunk), throwWhoosh,\n1342\trocketPass (a saw sweep), kickThump (sine 150→60 + noise), doink (a triangle 1400 ring), airHorn (3 saw voices\n1343\t220/277/330, 0.6 s), cannon (boom), fireworks (explode + crackle bursts), confettiPop, coinFlip (blip arpeggio),\n1344\tcardFlip, fiascoBreak (a descending arpeggio + wah), hotOn (a fire whoosh + a rising arpeggio), pileThumps, refCapPop,\n1345\tmascotSqueak, lightsOut (a power-down sweep), lightsOn (a thunk + hum), menuMove, menuSelect, menuBack, meterTick,\n1346\tmeterSet. Crowd one-shots: roar, boo, oooh, groan, gasp, cheer swell (bandpassed noise shapes on the crowd bus).\n1347\t\n1348\t### 13.3 The crowd bed\n1349\tA long-lived looping noise source on the `crowd` bus, through a bandpass (500–1500 Hz), with gain\n1350\t`0.12 + 0.38 × E` (E from §11.4), plus a lower \"murmur\" layer. Reactions are the one-shots above. Chants are 6\n1351\tdetuned babble voices in rhythm with the team's chant syllables (\"FI-AS-CO!\", \"HOGS! HOGS! HOGS!\", \"HOOT-HOOT!\",\n1352\t\"BRRR-BRRR!\", \"DY-NAS-TY!\"), on the beat (`audio.onBeat`) when E > 0.4. Stadium size scales it: Tumbleweed is thin\n1353\tand quiet (until the ending), the Mega Dome thick.\n1354\t\n1355\t### 13.4 Voices: the babble synth (Animalese and Simlish)\n1356\t**The API:** `FF.voice.say(text, profile, { onSyllable })` plans syllables, schedules them on the `voices` bus and\n1357\treturns the plan. `FF.voice.letter(ch, profile)` is the E5 dialog hook, which groups typed letters into syllables\n1358\tlive. `FF.voice.stop(profile)`.\n1359\t\n1360\t**Two styles, one synth:**\n1361\t- **Animalese** (the story cast, refs, mascot noises): letter-driven. Each vowel group in the typed text is one\n1362\t  syllable, with its onset taken from the preceding consonant(s). Quick, high and cute.\n1363\t- **Simlish** (the announcers, coaches on the radio, the crowd chants): count the line's syllables, then speak that\n1364\t  many syllables drawn from a gibberish bank (`ba do zee ka fla nu ree bo shma gli va tor mip zub lo neh`), picked by\n1365\t  a hash of each real word so the same line sounds the same every time. More fluent, with less pitch jitter.\n1366\t\n1367\t**A syllable** = onset + nucleus:\n1368\t- **Onset** by consonant class: plosives (p b t d k g c q) are an 8 ms broadband noise click; fricatives (f s z v h\n1369\t  x, sh, th) are 25–40 ms of bandpassed or highpassed noise (s at 5 kHz, f at 3 kHz, h at 1.2 kHz); nasals (m n) are\n1370\t  30 ms of lowpassed voiced hum; liquids (l r w y) are a formant glide into the vowel.\n1371\t- **Nucleus:** an oscillator (the profile's wave) through two parallel bandpass filters at the vowel's formants\n1372\t  (a 800/1200, e 500/1900, i 320/2300, o 500/900, u 350/800 Hz, × the profile's formant scale; Q 6 and 10), with an\n1373\t  envelope of 6 ms attack, a sustain, and a 25 ms release. The duration is (1 / rate) × 0.6–1.0.\n1374\t- **Pitch** = base × 2^((jitter + contour) / 12). The jitter is ±2 semitones from a hash of the syllable, so it's\n1375\t  deterministic. The contour: questions rise +4 on the last 2 syllables, exclamations +3 and +30% volume, statements\n1376\t  fall −2 at the end, ALL-CAPS words +2 and louder.\n1377\t- The voice is monophonic per speaker: a new syllable cuts the previous one. A lowpass at 3.5 kHz on the bus softens\n1378\t  it.\n1379\t\n1380\t**Speaker profiles** (`{ base Hz, rate syl/s, wave, formant, breath, style }`):\n1381\t\n1382\t| Speaker | Profile |\n1383\t|---|---|\n1384\t| CHET THUNDERBUNS | 125, 13, saw, 0.95, 0.05, simlish, excited contours |\n1385\t| LOU DIGGS | 105, 9, triangle, 0.9, 0.08, simlish, falling contours |\n1386\t| COACH GUS BLUSTER | 140, 14, square, 1.0, 0.15, animalese, shouty |\n1387\t| MAMA PEARL | 210, 10, triangle, 1.15, 0.05, animalese, warm |\n1388\t| HAP HAZARD | 165, 12, saw, 1.05, 0.03, animalese |\n1389\t| BROCK STERLING III | 150, 11, pulse, 1.0, 0.02, animalese, smug (rise then fall) |\n1390\t| MONTGOMERY STERLING II | 115, 8, saw, 0.92, 0.06, animalese, pompous |\n1391\t| BIG BERTHA | 190, 9, square, 1.1, 0.1, animalese |\n1392\t| GATOR GUIDRY | 120, 10, saw, 0.95, 0.12, animalese |\n1393\t| LUMEN LOPEZ | 140, 11, pulse, 1.0, 0.02, animalese |\n1394\t| ZAP ZAPATA | 190, 16, pulse12, 1.1, 0.0, animalese |\n1395\t| DJ HOOT | 130, 12, pulse, 1.0, 0.04, animalese, vibrato |\n1396\t| COACH ICEBERG | 100, 6, sine, 0.85, 0.05, animalese, slow and solemn |\n1397\t| AVALANCHE | growls only: lowpass noise + a 70 Hz sine, no syllables |\n1398\t| THE MOUNTAIN | 90, 7, square, 0.8, 0.1, animalese |\n1399\t| refs | 150, 12, square, 1.0, 0.0, animalese, short |\n1400\t| QBs | the QB's own profile, cadence \"SET... HUT!\" (2–4 syllables + a strong plosive \"HUT\" on the snap) |\n1401\t| crowd chant | 6 voices, ±15 cents detuned, simlish |\n1402\tVoices are on whenever text is spoken: subtitles always show. Settings: Voices volume (the `voices` bus), Commentary\n1403\t(On / Subtitles only / Off). Headless, the synth schedules nothing and returns the plan, so tests can check that\n1404\tsyllables were planned.\n1405\t\n1406\t---\n1407\t\n1408\t## 14. Screens and UX (10-foot, controller-first)\n1409\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c28e7abf-db6e-401a-a911-43ba9983a968","uuid":"15d16174-e646-498a-8342-4821f465253e","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:55:10.129Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174831__x898ed5/work/PLAN.md","content":"---\n\n## 13. Audio (`20-audio-voice.js`, `21-audio-music.js`)\nAll sound is synthesized: the engine's chip synth (`audio.sfx`, `audio.music`, `audio.define`) plus our Web Audio\nnodes on engine buses (E6). There are no audio files. Every composition is original: write new melodies, and\ndon't transcribe known stadium tunes or fight songs.\n\n### 13.1 Music (`{ bpm, steps, tracks }`, named tracks for layers)\n| Song | For | Character |\n|---|---|---|\n| FIASCO ANTHEM | the title, the story hub | stadium rock, 132 bpm: a square lead, saw bass, a `k . s .` beat, a cheer-like hook |\n| LOCKER ROOM FUNK | menus, setup | 110 bpm, a slap-bass `pulse12` line, offbeat hats |\n| HUDDLE UP | the play-call screen | a drumline loop (drums only plus a low triangle ostinato), 120 bpm, 2 bars, rising tension |\n| DUST DEVIL STOMP | Tumbleweed Field | a western shuffle, triangle bass, a twangy pulse lead, 128 bpm |\n| SWAMP STOMP | The Hog Wallow | a blues shuffle, saw bass, a harmonica-like pulse with vibrato, 96 bpm |\n| NEON NIGHTDRIVE | Afterglow Arena | synthwave, saw arpeggios, a square lead, 118 bpm |\n| FROSTBITE FANFARE | Frostbite Field | bells (triangle and sine arpeggios), 100 bpm |\n| MEGA DOME ANTHEM | the Mega Dome | epic brass (3 squares in fifths), 140 bpm |\n| HALFTIME SHOW | halftime | a marching band: `k s s` drums, a brass melody |\n| ARCADE ATTRACT | the attract mode | a 150 bpm remix of the anthem |\n| FIASCO FOREVER | the credits | a slow ballad version of the anthem |\n| Stings | `audio.sting` | TD fanfare, FG good, first-down organ riff, defense chant riff, Fiasco Break \"wah-wah\", victory jingle, defeat jingle (a sad sliding saw) |\n\nIn matches the stadium song plays low under the crowd bed, with layers `hot` (more drums and a guitar-like saw, for a\nBlazing athlete), `redzone` (bass pulses inside the 20) and `final` (`audio.tempo` +8% in the last minute). Between\nplays, original stadium-organ riffs (square and triangle chords with vibrato) play as stings on first downs, and a\ndefense-chant riff plays on 3rd down.\n\n### 13.2 Sound effects (`audio.define` each; the engine warns on unknown names, and a test fails on such a warning)\nhike (the QB's \"HUT\" voice + a snap thump), whistle, helmetClang (square 1800→1200 + a triangle ring), padsCrunch\n(lowpass noise 900→200 + sine 120→50), bigHit (crunch + clang + a boom sine 80→30), tackleThud, dive (whoosh),\nspinWhoosh, stiffArm (punch), hurdle (jump2), catchSlap (bandpass noise 1200, short), drop (thunk), throwWhoosh,\nrocketPass (a saw sweep), kickThump (sine 150→60 + noise), doink (a triangle 1400 ring), airHorn (3 saw voices\n220/277/330, 0.6 s), cannon (boom), fireworks (explode + crackle bursts), confettiPop, coinFlip (blip arpeggio),\ncardFlip, fiascoBreak (a descending arpeggio + wah), hotOn (a fire whoosh + a rising arpeggio), pileThumps, refCapPop,\nmascotSqueak, lightsOut (a power-down sweep), lightsOn (a thunk + hum), menuMove, menuSelect, menuBack, meterTick,\nmeterSet. Crowd one-shots: roar, boo, oooh, groan, gasp, cheer swell (bandpassed noise shapes on the crowd bus).\n\n### 13.3 The crowd bed\nA long-lived looping noise source on the `crowd` bus, through a bandpass (500–1500 Hz), with gain\n`0.12 + 0.38 × E` (E from §11.4), plus a lower \"murmur\" layer. Reactions are the one-shots above. Chants are 6\ndetuned babble voices in rhythm with the team's chant syllables (\"FI-AS-CO!\", \"HOGS! HOGS! HOGS!\", \"HOOT-HOOT!\",\n\"BRRR-BRRR!\", \"DY-NAS-TY!\"), on the beat (`audio.onBeat`) when E > 0.4. Stadium size scales it: Tumbleweed is thin\nand quiet (until the ending), the Mega Dome thick.\n\n### 13.4 Voices: the babble synth (Animalese and Simlish)\n**The API:** `FF.voice.say(text, profile, { onSyllable })` plans syllables, schedules them on the `voices` bus and\nreturns the plan. `FF.voice.letter(ch, profile)` is the E5 dialog hook, which groups typed letters into syllables\nlive. `FF.voice.stop(profile)`.\n\n**Two styles, one synth:**\n- **Animalese** (the story cast, refs, mascot noises): letter-driven. Each vowel group in the typed text is one\n  syllable, with its onset taken from the preceding consonant(s). Quick, high and cute.\n- **Simlish** (the announcers, coaches on the radio, the crowd chants): count the line's syllables, then speak that\n  many syllables drawn from a gibberish bank (`ba do zee ka fla nu ree bo shma gli va tor mip zub lo neh`), picked by\n  a hash of each real word so the same line sounds the same every time. More fluent, with less pitch jitter.\n\n**A syllable** = onset + nucleus:\n- **Onset** by consonant class: plosives (p b t d k g c q) are an 8 ms broadband noise click; fricatives (f s z v h\n  x, sh, th) are 25–40 ms of bandpassed or highpassed noise (s at 5 kHz, f at 3 kHz, h at 1.2 kHz); nasals (m n) are\n  30 ms of lowpassed voiced hum; liquids (l r w y) are a formant glide into the vowel.\n- **Nucleus:** an oscillator (the profile's wave) through two parallel bandpass filters at the vowel's formants\n  (a 800/1200, e 500/1900, i 320/2300, o 500/900, u 350/800 Hz, × the profile's formant scale; Q 6 and 10), with an\n  envelope of 6 ms attack, a sustain, and a 25 ms release. The duration is (1 / rate) × 0.6–1.0.\n- **Pitch** = base × 2^((jitter + contour) / 12). The jitter is ±2 semitones from a hash of the syllable, so it's\n  deterministic. The contour: questions rise +4 on the last 2 syllables, exclamations +3 and +30% volume, statements\n  fall −2 at the end, ALL-CAPS words +2 and louder.\n- The voice is monophonic per speaker: a new syllable cuts the previous one. A lowpass at 3.5 kHz on the bus softens\n  it.\n\n**Speaker profiles** (`{ base Hz, rate syl/s, wave, formant, breath, style }`):\n\n| Speaker | Profile |\n|---|---|\n| CHET THUNDERBUNS | 125, 13, saw, 0.95, 0.05, simlish, excited contours |\n| LOU DIGGS | 105, 9, triangle, 0.9, 0.08, simlish, falling contours |\n| COACH GUS BLUSTER | 140, 14, square, 1.0, 0.15, animalese, shouty |\n| MAMA PEARL | 210, 10, triangle, 1.15, 0.05, animalese, warm |\n| HAP HAZARD | 165, 12, saw, 1.05, 0.03, animalese |\n| BROCK STERLING III | 150, 11, pulse, 1.0, 0.02, animalese, smug (rise then fall) |\n| MONTGOMERY STERLING II | 115, 8, saw, 0.92, 0.06, animalese, pompous |\n| BIG BERTHA | 190, 9, square, 1.1, 0.1, animalese |\n| GATOR GUIDRY | 120, 10, saw, 0.95, 0.12, animalese |\n| LUMEN LOPEZ | 140, 11, pulse, 1.0, 0.02, animalese |\n| ZAP ZAPATA | 190, 16, pulse12, 1.1, 0.0, animalese |\n| DJ HOOT | 130, 12, pulse, 1.0, 0.04, animalese, vibrato |\n| COACH ICEBERG | 100, 6, sine, 0.85, 0.05, animalese, slow and solemn |\n| AVALANCHE | growls only: lowpass noise + a 70 Hz sine, no syllables |\n| THE MOUNTAIN | 90, 7, square, 0.8, 0.1, animalese |\n| refs | 150, 12, square, 1.0, 0.0, animalese, short |\n| QBs | the QB's own profile, cadence \"SET... HUT!\" (2–4 syllables + a strong plosive \"HUT\" on the snap) |\n| crowd chant | 6 voices, ±15 cents detuned, simlish |\nVoices are on whenever text is spoken: subtitles always show. Settings: Voices volume (the `voices` bus), Commentary\n(On / Subtitles only / Off). Headless, the synth schedules nothing and returns the plan, so tests can check that\nsyllables were planned.\n\n---\n\n## 14. Screens and UX (10-foot, controller-first)\n","numLines":100,"startLine":1310,"totalLines":2503}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01QhQc8pTqkaBkqPRwprURP7","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
